{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788291946686,"sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a09336d1f657bba5","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"git log --oneline -20 -- src/parse.rs"},"output":"d8f3c39 Merge main into gh:136 dsv branch (one-off landing, maintainer ruling 2026-09-01)\n55afe82 Track per-file definition origin and enforce visibility\nc73a4e4 Land issue-157: rust backend omits tl_fail helper for lines-only programs (gh:157)\n6d3f910 Add the parameterized dsv source, plus csv/tsv partials (gh:136)\n421450f Implement multi-line let bindings and the input type declaration the repros need\n8dc54ba Integrate issue-143 and issue-148: slices, and the vec-literal parser fix\n859bd55 Implement v[a:b] slices as index specs with jq clamping\n5928893 Fix vec literals being swallowed as indices after a definition\nbb44966 Add and, or, not as Bool keywords, splitting `or` by position\nec529ef Opt moves into the prelude: tagged absence, null only when printed\n06d383c Generic enums: parameters, instantiation, constructor inference\nf07d556 Nullary functions: fn name() -> T = body, called name() (issue #61)\n6f5e6fd Accept Opt<T> in the type grammar\n630a6fa Bare application becomes the default call form\n3c1e879 Match arms compose with or: guard arms, hybrid totality, // retires\nda1f20f Simplify check.rs per issue-4's style review\nec4632b Format the tree with rustfmt, once\n0dcdc5b Accept any single type as an enum payload, not only records\n232c152 Make Stream spellable in signatures, and make it linear\ncbc1e65 Reattach the doc comment bare_callee was inserted under\n","metadata":{"output":"d8f3c39 Merge main into gh:136 dsv branch (one-off landing, maintainer ruling 2026-09-01)\n55afe82 Track per-file definition origin and enforce visibility\nc73a4e4 Land issue-157: rust backend omits tl_fail helper for lines-only programs (gh:157)\n6d3f910 Add the parameterized dsv source, plus csv/tsv partials (gh:136)\n421450f Implement multi-line let bindings and the input type declaration the repros need\n8dc54ba Integrate issue-143 and issue-148: slices, and the vec-literal parser fix\n859bd55 Implement v[a:b] slices as index specs with jq clamping\n5928893 Fix vec literals being swallowed as indices after a definition\nbb44966 Add and, or, not as Bool keywords, splitting `or` by position\nec529ef Opt moves into the prelude: tagged absence, null only when printed\n06d383c Generic enums: parameters, instantiation, constructor inference\nf07d556 Nullary functions: fn name() -> T = body, called name() (issue #61)\n6f5e6fd Accept Opt<T> in the type grammar\n630a6fa Bare application becomes the default call form\n3c1e879 Match arms compose with or: guard arms, hybrid totality, // retires\nda1f20f Simplify check.rs per issue-4's style review\nec4632b Format the tree with rustfmt, once\n0dcdc5b Accept any single type as an enum payload, not only records\n232c152 Make Stream spellable in signatures, and make it linear\ncbc1e65 Reattach the doc comment bare_callee was inserted under\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"git log --oneline -20 -- src/parse.rs","time":{"start":1788291946684,"end":1788291946685}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The checker error message \"`{name}` is a function, not a value; write `{name}(...)` to call it\" is at line 1548. That's a different diagnostic. Let me understand: the brief says \"retiring the same-line heuristic (parser + checker + error message)\".\n\nWait — the brief says \"Implement the ruled change: an explicit terminator (;) after every fn/type declaration, retiring the same-line heuristic (parser + checker + error message).\"\n\nHmm, \"the same-line heuristic (parser + checker + error message)\". The parser has `same_line` method and `declined_cross_line`. The error message is the \"an argument must start on the same line as its function -- write `{callee}(...)` to call across lines\" message.\n\nLet me think carefully about what the ruled change means and how it interacts with the same-line rule.\n\nThe current grammar: a definition's body and the program's body sit adjacent with nothing between them. The line break keeps one from swallowing the other. This is the same-line rule for call arguments: an argument must start on the same line as its function. That's how `fn id(s: Str) -> Str = s` followed by `{a: 1}` on the next line works: `s` doesn't take `{a:1}` because it's on the next line.\n\nWith an explicit terminator `;` after every declaration, the boundary becomes a token. So after `fn id(s: Str) -> Str = s;`, the parser knows the def ended, and `{a: 1}` is the program body.\n\nNow the question is: does the same-line rule get fully retired, or only for the declaration boundary?\n\nLet me re-read the issue carefully:\n\n\"**Ruled: delimit declarations explicitly.** Replace the same-line heuristic (issues #10, #20) with a required terminator after every declaration -- the boundary becomes a token instead of a line-position fact.\"\n\n\"Implementation: pick the terminator token (semicolon is the working example in the grill round; confirm during implementation it doesn't collide with anything else in the grammar), require it after every `fn` and `type` declaration, and retire the same-line same-line rule and its diagnostic once the terminator makes the ambiguity unreachable. Update the corpus-pinned test (record_body_after_definition) and docs/reference/syntax accordingly.\"\n\nSo the plan:\n1. Add `;` terminator after every `fn` and `type` declaration. (What about `enum`? The issue says \"after every `fn` and `type` declaration\". Enums end with `}` so they're already delimited. But should enums get a terminator too? The issue says \"require it after every `fn` and `type` declaration\". Let me check the grammar. `enum Shape { point }` ends with `}` which is self-delimiting. So enums don't need a terminator. But wait — actually let me check whether enum declarations end with `}` and whether that's unambiguous. Yes, `enum_decl` ends with `}` via `eat(Tok::RBrace)`. So the `}` makes the enum self-delimiting. But fn declarations end with `= body` where body is an expression, which isn't self-delimiting. And `type X = ...` also isn't self-delimiting.\n\nWait, but actually `type` declarations — hmm, but the issue says \"require it after every `fn` and `type` declaration\". Let me check if there's a case where `type` needs it. `type Db = {users: Vec<User>}` ends with a type expr. The next declaration is `fn` or `type` etc. Actually, would a `type` declaration followed by another declaration be ambiguous without a terminator? Consider:\n```\ntype A = Int\ntype B = Str\n```\n`Int` then `type` — the parser reads `type_expr`, which reads `Int`, then the next token is `type`. That's fine actually since `type` can't continue a type expr. But what about `type A = {x: Int}` followed by... hmm.\n\nActually wait. Let me think about what makes the terminator necessary. The issue: `fn id(s: Str) -> Str = s` followed by `{a: 1}`. The body `s` swallows the record `{a: 1}` as a call. The terminator `;` fixes this: `fn id(s: Str) -> Str = s;` and then `{a: 1}` is the body.\n\nFor `type` declarations: `type Db = {users: Vec<User>}` — a record type followed by the program body `{a: 1}`... hmm, actually a record *type* `{users: Vec<User>}` — that ends with `}`. So the type is self-delimiting too? Let me think about `type A = Vec<Int>` followed by the program body. `Vec<Int>` is a type expr ending with `>`. Then the program body... would `>` be followed by an expression. Hmm.\n\nActually, let me not overthink. The ruling says \"require it after every `fn` and `type` declaration\". So I'll add `;` after both. Let me also consider whether the ruling intends enum to need it — \"every declaration\" but then \"require it after every `fn` and `type` declaration\" specifically. Enums end with `}` and are unambiguous. Actually wait — but there's a subtlety. Let me reconsider.\n\nHmm, actually let me re-read: \"an explicit terminator (;) after every fn/type declaration\". So `fn` and `type` only. Enums keep their `}`. That's the ruling as stated.\n\n2. Retire the same-line rule and its diagnostic once the terminator makes the ambiguity unreachable.\n\nSo now, the question is: what happens to the same-line rule for call arguments in general? The issue says \"retire the same-line rule and its diagnostic once the terminator makes the ambiguity unreachable.\"\n\nThe ambiguity in question is: a definition's body and the program's body sitting adjacent with nothing between them. With the terminator, this ambiguity is unreachable. So the same-line rule for call arguments can be retired entirely — arguments can start on any line.\n\nWait, but that's a bigger change. Let me think. Currently:\n- `takes_argument` returns whether the argument is on the same line, and records `declined_cross_line` if not.\n- If we retire the same-line rule, then `f(x)` and `f x` and `f {record}` can all span lines freely.\n\nBut wait — there's `declined_cross_line` which produces the diagnostic \"an argument must start on the same line as its function -- write `{callee}(...)` to call across lines\". This whole mechanism is what we retire.\n\nBut hold on — if we retire the same-line rule entirely, then a definition body could swallow the next declaration? No — because the terminator `;` ends the declaration. After `;`, the parser reads the next declaration (fn/type/enum/pub) or the program body.\n\nBut there's also the `input <type>` declaration handling that uses `same_line`: \"only when the type opens on `input`'s own line does the keyword start an annotation, so a body that merely uses `input` is never mistaken for one.\" Hmm. If we retire same_line, this needs a different disambiguation. Actually, `input` followed by a type on the same line... Let me think.\n\nActually wait. Let me re-read the brief from the user (the delegated worker instructions):\n\n\"Implement the ruled change: an explicit terminator (;) after every fn/type declaration, retiring the same-line heuristic (parser + checker + error message).\"\n\n\"retiring the same-line heuristic (parser + checker + error message)\" — so the same-line heuristic in the parser (the `same_line` method, `takes_argument`, `declined_cross_line`), in the checker (is there something in the checker?), and the error message.\n\nLet me look at the checker for the \"same line\" concept. The grep for \"same_line\" only found parse.rs and one comment in check/mod.rs:1042 about \"same linearity\" (that's different — about stream linearity).\n\nHmm, so what's in the checker? Let me search for the error message about cross-line in the checker. Actually, maybe the checker has a diagnostic about functions/arguments. Let me search more.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Let me look at git log / previous commits related to issues #10 and #20 to understand what the same-line heuristic was.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05e819772001C24ficonqOTy2R","sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","messageID":"msg_05e810101001aw6py4xUtsVpqh"}}
