{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788291950290,"sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","part":{"id":"prt_05e81a6ce001pKy1VyV11nbZcB","messageID":"msg_05e81a2c8001F5twVHxG5bDWL5","sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","snapshot":"0ebf070b795b2e106627c5d8e519929a4e05dbe7","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788291966777,"sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_01a05e81e6297fc19839d69d","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"git show 421450f --stat"},"output":"commit 421450f54397cbd1d261990129a8b1a5cbd6e345\nAuthor: Daniel Kantor <git@daniel-kantor.com>\nDate:   Sun Aug 30 23:59:16 2026 +0200\n\n    Implement multi-line let bindings and the input type declaration the repros need\n    \n    The let-sequence form (bindings one per line, then the block's value) lands in the parser, the checker, and the toylang emitter; each binding lowers to the same Kind::Bind a pipe does, so all seven backends run one without changes. The block is line-structured: a binding's value must fit its line, and the line that is not a let is the value.\n    \n    The lane brief blamed the scratch repros on this sequence swallowing the next line; it already stops at the newline. The real blocker is the program-body line 'input {x: Int, y: Int}', a typed-input declaration no toylang has ever parsed. 'input <type>' now declares what stdin holds, resolved into the same cell the uses of input read, so inference, validation, and every backend pick it up unchanged. A declared type must have a wire form (no Stream, Opt, Char, or Int64), and one that disagrees with a position expecting another type is the usual loud error.\n    \n    Derived: the 'input <type>' annotation, from the same-line rule a call argument follows, the checker's existing input cell, and input_read's wire refusals. The let-sequence form and its checker/emitter support came to this lane already written by the prior lane; not classified further.\n    \n    Written by DeepSeek V4 Flash via opencode.\n    \n    Co-Authored-By: DeepSeek V4 Flash via opencode\n\n site/public/corpus.json                            |  98 ++++++++++++++++++\n src/ast.rs                                         |  15 ++-\n src/check/mod.rs                                   | 112 +++++++++++++++++++--\n src/emit_toylang.rs                                |  27 +++++\n src/parse.rs                                       |  77 +++++++++++++-\n tests/corpus/input_annotation.yaml                 |  17 ++++\n tests/corpus/let_classify_cond.yaml                |  18 ++++\n tests/corpus/let_multi_input_annotation.yaml       |  21 ++++\n ...kend_llvm__native_agrees_where_it_compiles.snap |   5 +-\n ...ackend_rust__rust_agrees_where_it_compiles.snap |   5 +-\n .../step_2__concat_is_left_associative.snap        |   1 +\n 11 files changed, 385 insertions(+), 11 deletions(-)\n","metadata":{"output":"commit 421450f54397cbd1d261990129a8b1a5cbd6e345\nAuthor: Daniel Kantor <git@daniel-kantor.com>\nDate:   Sun Aug 30 23:59:16 2026 +0200\n\n    Implement multi-line let bindings and the input type declaration the repros need\n    \n    The let-sequence form (bindings one per line, then the block's value) lands in the parser, the checker, and the toylang emitter; each binding lowers to the same Kind::Bind a pipe does, so all seven backends run one without changes. The block is line-structured: a binding's value must fit its line, and the line that is not a let is the value.\n    \n    The lane brief blamed the scratch repros on this sequence swallowing the next line; it already stops at the newline. The real blocker is the program-body line 'input {x: Int, y: Int}', a typed-input declaration no toylang has ever parsed. 'input <type>' now declares what stdin holds, resolved into the same cell the uses of input read, so inference, validation, and every backend pick it up unchanged. A declared type must have a wire form (no Stream, Opt, Char, or Int64), and one that disagrees with a position expecting another type is the usual loud error.\n    \n    Derived: the 'input <type>' annotation, from the same-line rule a call argument follows, the checker's existing input cell, and input_read's wire refusals. The let-sequence form and its checker/emitter support came to this lane already written by the prior lane; not classified further.\n    \n    Written by DeepSeek V4 Flash via opencode.\n    \n    Co-Authored-By: DeepSeek V4 Flash via opencode\n\n site/public/corpus.json                            |  98 ++++++++++++++++++\n src/ast.rs                                         |  15 ++-\n src/check/mod.rs                                   | 112 +++++++++++++++++++--\n src/emit_toylang.rs                                |  27 +++++\n src/parse.rs                                       |  77 +++++++++++++-\n tests/corpus/input_annotation.yaml                 |  17 ++++\n tests/corpus/let_classify_cond.yaml                |  18 ++++\n tests/corpus/let_multi_input_annotation.yaml       |  21 ++++\n ...kend_llvm__native_agrees_where_it_compiles.snap |   5 +-\n ...ackend_rust__rust_agrees_where_it_compiles.snap |   5 +-\n .../step_2__concat_is_left_associative.snap        |   1 +\n 11 files changed, 385 insertions(+), 11 deletions(-)\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"git show 421450f --stat","time":{"start":1788291966774,"end":1788291966776}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I understand the history. The same-line rule was introduced in commit 630a6fa (issue #10, #20) and extended to indexing in 5928893 (issue #148).\n\nThe ruling now says: replace the same-line heuristic with an explicit terminator `;` after every `fn` and `type` declaration. Once the terminator makes the ambiguity unreachable, retire the same-line rule and its diagnostic.\n\nSo the plan:\n1. Add `;` token to the lexer.\n2. Require `;` after every `fn` and `type` declaration (in `parse` and `parse_module`).\n3. Retire the same-line rule: remove `same_line`, `takes_argument`, `declined_cross_line`, and the diagnostic. This means arguments can start on any line.\n4. Also retire the same-line rule for indexing (`[` on the same line only) — since the terminator makes the ambiguity unreachable. Wait, but the indexing rule was for the definition boundary too. With the terminator, the definition body ends at `;`, so `[` on the next line is the program body's vec literal. So the indexing same-line rule can also be retired.\n\nWait, but let me think about whether retiring the same-line rule entirely is correct. The issue says \"retire the same-line rule and its diagnostic once the terminator makes the ambiguity unreachable.\" The ambiguity is the definition-body/program-body boundary. With the terminator, that boundary is a token. So the same-line rule is no longer needed for that purpose.\n\nBut the same-line rule also serves other purposes? Let me think:\n- `takes_argument` for call arguments: with the terminator, a definition body ends at `;`, so a call argument on the next line can't swallow the program body. So the same-line rule for call arguments can be retired.\n- Indexing `[` on the same line: same reasoning.\n- The `input <type>` declaration: \"only when the type opens on `input`'s own line does the keyword start an annotation, so a body that merely uses `input` is never mistaken for one.\" Hmm. This is a different disambiguation. If we retire same_line, how do we disambiguate `input` as a value vs `input <type>` as a declaration?\n\nLet me think about this. `input` is a keyword that can be:\n1. A value (the stdin value), used in expressions.\n2. A declaration: `input <type>` at the top of the program, after the defs.\n\nThe current rule: `input` followed by `{` or an identifier on the same line is a declaration. Otherwise it's a value.\n\nIf we retire same_line, we need another way. Hmm. Actually, wait — the `input` declaration is read in `parse` after the defs loop. The defs loop breaks when it sees a token that's not `fn`/`pub`/`type`/`enum`. So `input` breaks the loop. Then the `input` handling checks if the next token is `{` or an identifier on the same line.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. With the terminator, the defs loop would break at `;`? No — the defs loop reads `fn`/`pub`/`type`/`enum` declarations. After each declaration, we'd eat the `;`. Then the loop continues. When it hits `input`, it breaks. Then the input handling.\n\nHmm, but the `input <type>` disambiguation: `input` followed by `{` or `Ident` on the same line. If we retire same_line, then `input` followed by `{` on the next line... would that be a declaration or a value? \n\nActually, let me look at how `input` is used. In the program body, `input` is a value. The declaration `input <type>` sits at the top. The disambiguation matters because a program body could start with `input` as a value, e.g.:\n```\nfn f(x: Int) -> Int = x\ninput\n```\nHere `input` is the program body (a value). And:\n```\ninput Int\nfn f(x: Int) -> Int = x\n```\nHmm wait, no. The `input` declaration is read after the defs. Let me re-read the parse function.\n\n```rust\nloop {\n    let (tok, _) = p.peek()?;\n    match tok {\n        Tok::Pub => { ... }\n        Tok::Fn => defs.push(p.def(false)?),\n        Tok::Type => aliases.push(p.alias()?),\n        Tok::Enum => enums.push(p.enum_decl(false)?),\n        _ => break,\n    }\n}\n\nlet input = match p.peek()? {\n    (Tok::Input, input_span) => {\n        let (next, next_span) = p.peek2()?;\n        if (next == Tok::LBrace || matches!(next, Tok::Ident(_)))\n            && p.same_line(input_span.end, next_span.start)\n        {\n            p.advance()?;\n            Some(p.type_expr()?)\n        } else {\n            None\n        }\n    }\n    _ => None,\n};\n\nlet body = p.expr(0)?;\n```\n\nSo the `input` declaration is only recognized when `input` is followed by `{` or an identifier on the same line. Otherwise `input` is treated as the start of the program body (a value).\n\nNow, if we retire same_line, we need to decide: is `input` followed by `{` or `Ident` a declaration regardless of line? Consider:\n```\nfn f(x: Int) -> Int = x\ninput\n{a: 1}\n```\nHere `input` is a value, and `{a: 1}` is... wait, `input` followed by `{a: 1}` on the next line. If we treat `input {` as a declaration regardless of line, then `input {a: 1}` would be parsed as `input` declared as record type `{a: 1}`. That would be wrong — the user meant `input` as a value and `{a: 1}` as... hmm, actually `input` followed by `{a: 1}` — is that a call? No, `input` is not a function. Actually `input {a: 1}` — hmm.\n\nWait, actually, let me reconsider. Is `input` a value that can be followed by a record literal? `input` is a value. `input {a: 1}` — that would be... `input` is not a function, so it can't be called. So `input {a: 1}` as an expression would be a parse error (\"expected an expression, found `{`\" or similar). Hmm, actually no. Let me think about the grammar. `input` is an atom. After `input`, the postfix loop checks for `[`, `!`, `.`. `{` is not handled, so `input` returns as a Var-like expr. Then the enclosing expression sees `{` and... it's not an infix operator, so the expression ends. Then the program body parse would fail with \"expected end of program, found `{`\".\n\nHmm wait, but actually — is `input {a: 1}` a call? No. `input` is a keyword, not a function name. So `input {a: 1}` is not a call.\n\nOK here's the thing. The `input <type>` declaration disambiguation is genuinely tricky. Let me look at how the docs describe it and whether there are corpus cases.\n\nActually, let me step back and think about the scope of the change. The ruling says:\n\n\"require it after every `fn` and `type` declaration, and retire the same-line rule and its diagnostic once the terminator makes the ambiguity unreachable.\"\n\nThe ambiguity that the terminator makes unreachable is the definition-body/program-body boundary. The same-line rule was introduced to fix that (issues #10, #20). So retiring the same-line rule means: call arguments can start on any line now, because the terminator handles the boundary.\n\nBut the `input <type>` disambiguation is a separate use of `same_line`. Hmm. Let me check: was the `input` same-line check part of the same-line rule introduced in #10/#20, or was it added later?\n\nLet me look at the commit that added the `input` typedeclaration.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05e81e69b0017cHFNbmsUmxEGR","sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","messageID":"msg_05e81a2c8001F5twVHxG5bDWL5"}}
