{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788291985143,"sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_01a05e822da97dc2a3cd38cd","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/docs/reference/sources/input.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/docs/reference/sources/input.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # input\n2: \n3: `input` is stdin as one JSON value, read whole. It has no type of its own: it is checked\n4: against whatever the position expects, first use wins, so it is normally handed straight to\n5: a function whose parameter type says what stdin must be.\n6: \n7: ```case\n8: adults\n9: ```\n10: \n11: Before the program runs, the value is validated against that type -- a parse, not a\n12: coercion. `{\"age\": \"36\"}` where `Int` was declared is an error, not a conversion; a number\n13: that does not fit in 32 bits is refused; a missing declared field is refused; undeclared\n14: fields are ignored. See [records as input](../types/record.md) and\n15: [enums as input](../types/enum.md).\n16: \n17: Any position that expects a type can supply `input`'s -- an argument, an annotated function\n18: body, a record field checked against a declared record type. First use wins, and it wins for\n19: every later use too: once one position has typed `input`, a use where nothing expects\n20: anything means the same value at the same type.\n21: \n22: ```case\n23: input_typed_by_first_use\n24: ```\n25: \n26: A program whose only use of `input` sits where nothing expects anything is still refused\n27: with \"cannot tell what `input` contains\": there is no first use to borrow from.\n28: \n29: `input` reads the same real stdin as [`inputs`](inputs.md), [`lines`](lines.md), and\n30: [`dsv`](dsv.md), so a program uses at most one of the four: any two together are\n31: refused, because they would read one resource two different ways.\n\n(End of file - total 31 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# input\n\n`input` is stdin as one JSON value, read whole. It has no type of its own: it is checked\nagainst whatever the position expects, first use wins, so it is normally handed straight to\na function whose parameter type says what stdin must be.\n\n```case\nadults\n```\n\nBefore the program runs, the value is validated against that type -- a parse, not a\ncoercion. `{\"age\": \"36\"}` where `Int` was declared is an error, not a conversion; a number\nthat does not fit in 32 bits is refused; a missing declared field is refused; undeclared\nfields are ignored. See [records as input](../types/record.md) and\n[enums as input](../types/enum.md).\n\nAny position that expects a type can supply `input`'s -- an argument, an annotated function\nbody, a record field checked against a declared record type. First use wins, and it wins for\nevery later use too: once one position has typed `input`, a use where nothing expects\nanything means the same value at the same type.","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/docs/reference/sources/input.md","text":"# input\n\n`input` is stdin as one JSON value, read whole. It has no type of its own: it is checked\nagainst whatever the position expects, first use wins, so it is normally handed straight to\na function whose parameter type says what stdin must be.\n\n```case\nadults\n```\n\nBefore the program runs, the value is validated against that type -- a parse, not a\ncoercion. `{\"age\": \"36\"}` where `Int` was declared is an error, not a conversion; a number\nthat does not fit in 32 bits is refused; a missing declared field is refused; undeclared\nfields are ignored. See [records as input](../types/record.md) and\n[enums as input](../types/enum.md).\n\nAny position that expects a type can supply `input`'s -- an argument, an annotated function\nbody, a record field checked against a declared record type. First use wins, and it wins for\nevery later use too: once one position has typed `input`, a use where nothing expects\nanything means the same value at the same type.\n\n```case\ninput_typed_by_first_use\n```\n\nA program whose only use of `input` sits where nothing expects anything is still refused\nwith \"cannot tell what `input` contains\": there is no first use to borrow from.\n\n`input` reads the same real stdin as [`inputs`](inputs.md), [`lines`](lines.md), and\n[`dsv`](dsv.md), so a program uses at most one of the four: any two together are\nrefused, because they would read one resource two different ways.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":31,"totalLines":31,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/reference/sources/input.md","time":{"start":1788291985132,"end":1788291985139}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I understand the corpus cases. Let me think about the design.\n\nThe ruling: add `;` after every `fn` and `type` declaration. Retire the same-line rule and its diagnostic.\n\nNow, the `input <type>` declaration. The current disambiguation uses same_line. If we retire same_line, we need to handle this. Let me think about what the right approach is.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider whether the `input <type>` disambiguation needs the same-line rule at all. The `input` declaration is read after the defs loop. The defs loop breaks at `input`. So `input` at the top of the program (after defs) is either:\n1. The `input <type>` declaration, or\n2. The start of the program body (a value).\n\nThe current rule: `input` followed by `{` or `Ident` on the same line → declaration. Otherwise → value.\n\nIf we remove the same-line check, then `input` followed by `{` or `Ident` (on any line) → declaration. \n\nIs there a real program where `input` as a value is followed by `{` or an identifier on the next line? Let me think:\n- `input` as a value followed by `{...}` on the next line: `input\\n{a: 1}`. As discussed, this is already a parse error in the current grammar (two expressions in a row). So treating it as a declaration changes the error message but doesn't break a valid program.\n- `input` as a value followed by an identifier on the next line: `input\\nf(x)`. Hmm, `input` followed by `f(x)` — is that a call? No, `input` is not a function. So `input\\nf(x)` is also a parse error currently.\n\nWait, but actually — hold on. Is `input` a value that could be followed by a bare call? No. `input` is an atom. After `input`, the postfix loop handles `[`, `!`, `.`. Then the expression ends. So `input` followed by anything that's not an infix operator or postfix is a parse error at the program level.\n\nHmm, but wait — what about `input` as a value in the middle of an expression? Like `f(input)`? That's fine, `input` is inside parens. The `input <type>` declaration only appears at the top level after defs.\n\nSo actually, the same-line check for `input` might not be strictly necessary. But removing it changes behavior: `input\\n{...}` would become a declaration attempt instead of a parse error. Let me think about whether that matters.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The brief says \"retiring the same-line heuristic (parser + checker + error message)\". The `input` same-line check is part of the same-line heuristic (it was \"derived from the same-line rule a call argument follows\"). So I should retire it too.\n\nBut what replaces it? Let me think about the cleanest approach. With the terminator, the program structure is:\n\n```\n<defs, each ending with ;>\n[input <type>;]  -- hmm, does input get a terminator?\n<body>\n```\n\nWait, does the `input <type>` declaration get a terminator? The ruling says \"after every `fn` and `type` declaration\". The `input` declaration is not a `fn` or `type` declaration. Hmm. But it's a declaration. Let me think.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The `input <type>` declaration is read after the defs loop. If it doesn't get a terminator, then how does the parser know where it ends? `input {x: Int, y: Int}` — the type expr `{x: Int, y: Int}` is self-delimiting (ends with `}`). `input Int` — `Int` is a named type, self-delimiting. So the `input` declaration is self-delimiting by its type. But then the body follows. `input Int\\nf(input)` — after `Int`, the next token is `f`. The body starts at `f`. That works without a terminator.\n\nBut wait — what if the body starts with something that could continue the type? Like `input Int` followed by a body that starts with... hmm, `Int` is a complete type. The body would start with an expression. Could an expression continue a type? No, types and expressions are disjoint token-wise mostly. Actually `input Vec<Int>` — after `>`, the body starts. Fine.\n\nHmm, but actually, should `input <type>` get a `;` too? Let me look at the docs for input to see the current syntax.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05e822e1d001OuIhjdEETy0k5I","sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","messageID":"msg_05e8208fb001I0i5XE3TsNj6Iq"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788291985326,"sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_01a05e822e8e7993a3735ea2","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/docs/reference/syntax/programs.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/docs/reference/syntax/programs.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # A program\n2: \n3: A program is declarations followed by one expression, and the expression's value is what\n4: prints. There are no statements, no `main`, and no print call: output needs no side effect,\n5: because the program is the expression.\n6: \n7: ```toylang\n8: # Comments start with `#` and run to the end of the line.\n9: type Pair = {a: Int, b: Int}\n10: \n11: fn total(p: Pair) -> Int = p.a + p.b\n12: \n13: {sum: total({a: 1, b: 2})}\n14: ```\n15: \n16: ```output\n17: {\"sum\":3}\n18: ```\n19: \n20: The declarations are [type aliases](../types/alias.md), [enums](../types/enum.md), and\n21: [functions](functions.md), in any order; a function may call one defined later. `pub` on a\n22: `fn` marks it for a future module story and currently changes nothing in a program's own\n23: file.\n24: \n25: How the value prints: a top-level `Str` prints raw; everything else prints as compact JSON,\n26: with record fields in the order their type declares them. A program whose outermost\n27: expression is the\n28: [`jsonlines`](../builtins/jsonlines.md) sink prints line by line instead and has no value.\n29: \n30: Name casing is a rule, not a convention: a capitalized name is a type, a lowercase name is\n31: a value. Field and variant names come from data and are exempt.\n32: \n33: Everything is checked before anything runs: types, match coverage, stream linearity, the\n34: 32-bit literal rule. What remains at runtime is the arithmetic refusals (`/ 0`, `% 0`),\n35: absent-value unwraps, and input that misses its declared type.\n\n(End of file - total 35 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# A program\n\nA program is declarations followed by one expression, and the expression's value is what\nprints. There are no statements, no `main`, and no print call: output needs no side effect,\nbecause the program is the expression.\n\n```toylang\n# Comments start with `#` and run to the end of the line.\ntype Pair = {a: Int, b: Int}\n\nfn total(p: Pair) -> Int = p.a + p.b\n\n{sum: total({a: 1, b: 2})}\n```\n\n```output\n{\"sum\":3}\n```\n\nThe declarations are [type aliases](../types/alias.md), [enums](../types/enum.md), and","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/docs/reference/syntax/programs.md","text":"# A program\n\nA program is declarations followed by one expression, and the expression's value is what\nprints. There are no statements, no `main`, and no print call: output needs no side effect,\nbecause the program is the expression.\n\n```toylang\n# Comments start with `#` and run to the end of the line.\ntype Pair = {a: Int, b: Int}\n\nfn total(p: Pair) -> Int = p.a + p.b\n\n{sum: total({a: 1, b: 2})}\n```\n\n```output\n{\"sum\":3}\n```\n\nThe declarations are [type aliases](../types/alias.md), [enums](../types/enum.md), and\n[functions](functions.md), in any order; a function may call one defined later. `pub` on a\n`fn` marks it for a future module story and currently changes nothing in a program's own\nfile.\n\nHow the value prints: a top-level `Str` prints raw; everything else prints as compact JSON,\nwith record fields in the order their type declares them. A program whose outermost\nexpression is the\n[`jsonlines`](../builtins/jsonlines.md) sink prints line by line instead and has no value.\n\nName casing is a rule, not a convention: a capitalized name is a type, a lowercase name is\na value. Field and variant names come from data and are exempt.\n\nEverything is checked before anything runs: types, match coverage, stream linearity, the\n32-bit literal rule. What remains at runtime is the arithmetic refusals (`/ 0`, `% 0`),\nabsent-value unwraps, and input that misses its declared type.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":35,"totalLines":35,"truncated":false}},"title":"docs/reference/syntax/programs.md","time":{"start":1788291985316,"end":1788291985325}},"id":"prt_05e822f04001SnhG4ybjBPoDq2","sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","messageID":"msg_05e8208fb001I0i5XE3TsNj6Iq"}}
