{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788292033080,"sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","part":{"id":"prt_05e82ea35001TubFWHao55b0HL","messageID":"msg_05e82e621001zD6KxqzUNLmvpB","sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","snapshot":"0ebf070b795b2e106627c5d8e519929a4e05dbe7","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788292033631,"sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_01a05e82eb3d7282a80715ae","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/tests/docs.rs"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/tests/docs.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: //! The docs harness: every code fragment in `docs/**/*.md` is a real program.\n2: //!\n3: //! A fragment is a `toylang` fence, an `input` fence if the program reads stdin,\n4: //! and exactly one of an `output`, `refuses`, or `error` fence. The first two go through the\n5: //! same seven-backend agreement check as the corpus, because a docs fragment is a corpus case\n6: //! defined in prose; an `error` fence pins the checker's message for a program the docs show\n7: //! being turned away. A page can also embed an existing corpus case by id with a `case` fence\n8: //! instead of repeating its program.\n9: //!\n10: //! What this buys is that the documentation cannot lie: a claim about what a program prints is\n11: //! run, not proofread, and drift between the docs and the compiler fails `just test`.\n12: //!\n13: //! There is no way for a fragment to opt out of that. A `fixture` fence used to name a\n14: //! gitignored path for stdin that could not be committed, skipping the fragment when the file\n15: //! was absent, which it was for everyone; the four pages using it published an answer nobody\n16: //! here had run. A gate that skips silently reads exactly like a check that passed. A claim this\n17: //! harness cannot run belongs in an issue, not in an `output` fence\n18: //! ([kantord/toylang#129](https://github.com/kantord/toylang/issues/129)).\n19: //!\n20: //! `toylang slow` is the one deliberate exception, and it is not a skip. A `slow` fragment is\n21: //! an `output` fragment that the default run only type-checks and emits on every backend -- it\n22: //! still rots the moment the language stops accepting it -- and executes on every backend only\n23: //! when asked to, `TOYLANG_SLOW=1 just test` (or the `just slow-test` recipe). The tier is for\n24: //! programs that are verified but take the interpreted backends minutes to run, currently Euler\n25: //! 10 and 14 ([kantord/toylang#90](https://github.com/kantord/toylang/issues/90)); it is for\n26: //! measured cost, not impatience, so a fragment only earns it by being slow in fact.\n27: \n28: mod support;\n29: \n30: use std::path::{Path, PathBuf};\n31: \n32: use support::Expect;\n33: \n34: /// What a fragment claims happens to its program.\n35: enum Outcome {\n36:     /// Compiles, runs on every backend, and they all print this.\n37:     Output(String),\n38:     /// Compiles, and every backend refuses to run it. The fence is empty: what each backend\n39:     /// says while refusing is its own business, so there is no text to pin.\n40:     Refusal,\n41:     /// Does not compile, and this is the checker's message, verbatim.\n42:     Error(String),\n43: }\n44: \n45: struct Fragment {\n46:     /// `page:line` of the opening fence, so a failure points at the prose to fix.\n47:     at: String,\n48:     program: String,\n49:     input: Option<String>,\n50:     outcome: Outcome,\n51:     /// A `toylang slow` fence: type-checked and emitted on every backend in the default run,\n52:     /// executed on every backend only under `TOYLANG_SLOW=1`.\n53:     slow: bool,\n54: }\n55: \n56: /// A `toylang` fence whose expectation fence has not arrived yet.\n57: struct Pending {\n58:     at: String,\n59:     program: String,\n60:     input: Option<String>,\n61:     slow: bool,\n62: }\n63: \n64: /// Fills in a pending fragment's stdin, from an `input` fence: refuses a second one, and a\n65: /// fence with no fragment open to receive it.\n66: fn set_pending_input(pending: &mut Option<Pending>, at: &str, body: String) {\n67:     match pending {\n68:         Some(p) if p.input.is_none() => p.input = Some(body),\n69:         Some(Pending { at: prev, .. }) => panic!(\"{at}: fragment at {prev} already has an input\"),\n70:         None => panic!(\"{at}: `input` fence with no `toylang` fence before it\"),\n71:     }\n72: }\n73: \n74: #[test]\n75: fn every_fragment_is_a_real_program() {\n76:     let mut fragments = Vec::new();\n77:     let mut embedded = Vec::new();\n78:     for (page, text) in pages() {\n79:         extract(&page, &text, &mut fragments, &mut embedded);\n80:     }\n81:     assert!(\n82:         !fragments.is_empty(),\n83:         \"the docs have no fragments, so this test proves nothing\"\n84:     );\n85:     assert!(\n86:         fragments.iter().any(|f| f.slow),\n87:         \"no docs fragment is marked `slow`, so the tier proves nothing; delete it\"\n88:     );\n89: \n90:     let mut failures = Vec::new();\n91: \n92:     for f in &fragments {\n93:         if f.slow && !slow_runs_enabled() {\n94:             failures.extend(compile_only_failures(f));\n95:             continue;\n96:         }\n97:         match &f.outcome {\n98:             Outcome::Output(want) => failures.extend(support::agreement_failures(\n99:                 &f.at,\n100:                 &f.program,\n101:                 f.input.as_deref(),\n102:                 &Expect::Output(want.clone()),\n103:             )),\n104:             Outcome::Refusal => {\n105:                 // Compiling is checked first and separately: to `agreement_failures` a program\n106:                 // that does not compile looks exactly like one every backend refused.\n107:                 if let Err(e) = toylang::compile(&f.program) {\n108:                     failures.push(format!(\"BROKEN  {}: does not compile: {e}\", f.at));\n109:                 } else {\n110:                     failures.extend(support::agreement_failures(\n111:                         &f.at,\n112:                         &f.program,\n113:                         f.input.as_deref(),\n114:                         &Expect::Refusal,\n115:                     ));\n116:                 }\n117:             }\n118:             Outcome::Error(want) => match toylang::compile(&f.program) {\n119:                 Ok(_) => failures.push(format!(\n120:                     \"COMPILED {}: claims `{want}` but the program compiles\",\n121:                     f.at\n122:                 )),\n123:                 Err(e) if e.to_string() != *want => failures.push(format!(\n124:                     \"MISQUOTED {}: the checker says {:?}, the page says {want:?}\",\n125:                     f.at,\n126:                     e.to_string()\n127:                 )),\n128:                 Err(_) => {}\n129:             },\n130:         }\n131:     }\n132: \n133:     for (at, id) in &embedded {\n134:         if !support::dir().join(format!(\"{id}.yaml\")).is_file() {\n135:             failures.push(format!(\n136:                 \"MISSING {at}: embeds corpus case `{id}`, which does not exist\"\n137:             ));\n138:         }\n139:     }\n140: \n141:     assert!(\n142:         failures.is_empty(),\n143:         \"{} of {} docs fragments failed:\\n{}\",\n144:         failures.len(),\n145:         fragments.len(),\n146:         failures.join(\"\\n\")\n147:     );\n148: }\n149: \n150: /// Whether the slow tier executes. `TOYLANG_SLOW=1` turns it on; the default run compiles a\n151: /// `slow` fragment on every backend without running it.\n152: fn slow_runs_enabled() -> bool {\n153:     std::env::var(\"TOYLANG_SLOW\").is_ok_and(|v| v == \"1\")\n154: }\n155: \n156: /// The default check for a `slow` fragment. Running it is what costs minutes, so the default\n157: /// run proves only what is cheap: it parses, type-checks, and emits on every backend. The\n158: /// agreement and the printed answer are the opt-in run's claim.\n159: fn compile_only_failures(f: &Fragment) -> Vec<String> {\n160:     let program = match toylang::compile(&f.program) {\n161:         Ok(p) => p,\n162:         Err(e) => return vec![format!(\"BROKEN  {}: does not compile: {e}\", f.at)],\n163:     };\n164:     let mut failures = Vec::new();\n165:     for backend in toylang::Backend::ALL {\n166:         if let Err(e) = backend.emit(&program) {\n167:             failures.push(format!(\n168:                 \"BROKEN  {}: {} could not emit: {e}\",\n169:                 f.at,\n170:                 backend.name()\n171:             ));\n172:         }\n173:     }\n174:     failures\n175: }\n176: \n177: /// The reference is complete for everything implemented, checkably: a builtin without a page\n178: /// fails here, the same way a corpus case without fresh tags fails tag_corpus.\n179: #[test]\n180: fn every_builtin_has_a_reference_page() {\n181:     let missing: Vec<&str> = toylang::check::BUILTIN_NAMES\n182:         .into_iter()\n183:         .filter(|name| {\n184:             !docs_dir()\n185:                 .join(\"reference/builtins\")\n186:                 .join(format!(\"{name}.md\"))\n187:                 .is_file()\n188:         })\n189:         .collect();\n190:     assert!(\n191:         missing.is_empty(),\n192:         \"builtins without a reference page under docs/reference/builtins/: {}\",\n193:         missing.join(\", \")\n194:     );\n195: }\n196: \n197: fn repo_root() -> PathBuf {\n198:     Path::new(env!(\"CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR\")).to_path_buf()\n199: }\n200: \n201: fn docs_dir() -> PathBuf {\n202:     repo_root().join(\"docs\")\n203: }\n204: \n205: /// Every markdown page under `docs/`, as (path relative to the repo, content), sorted so\n206: /// failures come out in a stable order.\n207: fn pages() -> Vec<(String, String)> {\n208:     let mut paths = Vec::new();\n209:     walk(&docs_dir(), &mut paths);\n210:     paths.sort();\n211:     paths\n212:         .into_iter()\n213:         .map(|p| {\n214:             let text =\n215:                 std::fs::read_to_string(&p).unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!(\"{}: {e}\", p.display()));\n216:             let rel = p\n217:                 .strip_prefix(Path::new(env!(\"CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR\")))\n218:                 .expect(\"under the repo\")\n219:                 .to_string_lossy()\n220:                 .into_owned();\n221:             (rel, text)\n222:         })\n223:         .collect()\n224: }\n225: \n226: fn walk(dir: &Path, out: &mut Vec<PathBuf>) {\n227:     for entry in\n228:         std::fs::read_dir(dir).unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!(\"cannot read {}: {e}\", dir.display()))\n229:     {\n230:         let path = entry.expect(\"readable entry\").path();\n231:         if path.is_dir() {\n232:             walk(&path, out);\n233:         } else if path.extension().is_some_and(|e| e == \"md\") {\n234:             out.push(path);\n235:         }\n236:     }\n237: }\n238: \n239: /// Pulls the fragments out of one page. Malformed structure -- an `input` with no program, a\n240: /// program left without an expectation -- is a panic naming the line, like a malformed corpus\n241: /// case: a fence that asks for nothing looks exactly like a fence that passes.\n242: fn extract(\n243:     page: &str,\n244:     text: &str,\n245:     fragments: &mut Vec<Fragment>,\n246:     embedded: &mut Vec<(String, String)>,\n247: ) {\n248:     // A `toylang` fence whose expectation fence has not arrived yet.\n249:     let mut pending: Option<Pending> = None;\n250:     let mut lines = text.lines().enumerate().peekable();\n251: \n252:     while let Some((i, line)) = lines.next() {\n253:         let Some(info) = line.strip_prefix(\"```\") else {\n254:             continue;\n255:         };\n256:         let at = format!(\"{page}:{}\", i + 1);\n257:         let mut body = String::new();\n258:         loop {\n259:             match lines.next() {\n260:                 Some((_, \"```\")) => break,\n261:                 Some((_, l)) => {\n262:                     body.push_str(l);\n263:                     body.push('\\n');\n264:                 }\n265:                 None => panic!(\"{at}: fence never closes\"),\n266:             }\n267:         }\n268:         match info.trim() {\n269:             \"toylang\" | \"toylang slow\" => {\n270:                 if let Some(Pending { at: prev, .. }) = &pending {\n271:                     panic!(\"{prev}: fragment has no `output`, `refuses`, or `error` fence\");\n272:                 }\n273:                 pending = Some(Pending {\n274:                     at,\n275:                     program: body,\n276:                     input: None,\n277:                     slow: info.trim() == \"toylang slow\",\n278:                 });\n279:             }\n280:             \"input\" => set_pending_input(&mut pending, &at, body),\n281:             \"output\" | \"refuses\" | \"error\" => {\n282:                 let Some(p) = pending.take() else {\n283:                     panic!(\n284:                         \"{at}: `{}` fence with no `toylang` fence before it\",\n285:                         info.trim()\n286:                     )\n287:                 };\n288:                 assert!(\n289:                     !p.slow || info.trim() == \"output\",\n290:                     \"{at}: a `slow` fragment runs on every backend and agrees, so it needs an \\\n291:                      `output` fence\"\n292:                 );\n293:                 let outcome = match info.trim() {\n294:                     \"output\" => Outcome::Output(body),\n295:                     \"error\" => Outcome::Error(body.trim_end().to_string()),\n296:                     _ => {\n297:                         assert!(\n298:                             body.is_empty(),\n299:                             \"{at}: a `refuses` fence is empty; what each backend says while \\\n300:                              refusing is its own business\"\n301:                         );\n302:                         Outcome::Refusal\n303:                     }\n304:                 };\n305:                 fragments.push(Fragment {\n306:                     at: p.at,\n307:                     program: p.program,\n308:                     input: p.input,\n309:                     outcome,\n310:                     slow: p.slow,\n311:                 });\n312:             }\n313:             \"case\" => {\n314:                 let id = body.trim();\n315:                 assert!(\n316:                     !id.is_empty() && !id.contains(char::is_whitespace),\n317:                     \"{at}: a `case` fence holds one corpus case id\"\n318:                 );\n319:                 embedded.push((at, id.to_string()));\n320:             }\n321:             // Any other language is ordinary illustration, not a claim about the compiler.\n322:             _ => {}\n323:         }\n324:     }\n325:     if let Some(Pending { at: prev, .. }) = pending {\n326:         panic!(\"{prev}: fragment has no `output`, `refuses`, or `error` fence\");\n327:     }\n328: }\n\n(End of file - total 328 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"//! The docs harness: every code fragment in `docs/**/*.md` is a real program.\n//!\n//! A fragment is a `toylang` fence, an `input` fence if the program reads stdin,\n//! and exactly one of an `output`, `refuses`, or `error` fence. The first two go through the\n//! same seven-backend agreement check as the corpus, because a docs fragment is a corpus case\n//! defined in prose; an `error` fence pins the checker's message for a program the docs show\n//! being turned away. A page can also embed an existing corpus case by id with a `case` fence\n//! instead of repeating its program.\n//!\n//! What this buys is that the documentation cannot lie: a claim about what a program prints is\n//! run, not proofread, and drift between the docs and the compiler fails `just test`.\n//!\n//! There is no way for a fragment to opt out of that. A `fixture` fence used to name a\n//! gitignored path for stdin that could not be committed, skipping the fragment when the file\n//! was absent, which it was for everyone; the four pages using it published an answer nobody\n//! here had run. A gate that skips silently reads exactly like a check that passed. A claim this\n//! harness cannot run belongs in an issue, not in an `output` fence\n//! ([kantord/toylang#129](https://github.com/kantord/toylang/issues/129)).\n//!\n//! `toylang slow` is the one deliberate exception, and it is not a skip. A `slow` fragment is","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/tests/docs.rs","text":"//! The docs harness: every code fragment in `docs/**/*.md` is a real program.\n//!\n//! A fragment is a `toylang` fence, an `input` fence if the program reads stdin,\n//! and exactly one of an `output`, `refuses`, or `error` fence. The first two go through the\n//! same seven-backend agreement check as the corpus, because a docs fragment is a corpus case\n//! defined in prose; an `error` fence pins the checker's message for a program the docs show\n//! being turned away. A page can also embed an existing corpus case by id with a `case` fence\n//! instead of repeating its program.\n//!\n//! What this buys is that the documentation cannot lie: a claim about what a program prints is\n//! run, not proofread, and drift between the docs and the compiler fails `just test`.\n//!\n//! There is no way for a fragment to opt out of that. A `fixture` fence used to name a\n//! gitignored path for stdin that could not be committed, skipping the fragment when the file\n//! was absent, which it was for everyone; the four pages using it published an answer nobody\n//! here had run. A gate that skips silently reads exactly like a check that passed. A claim this\n//! harness cannot run belongs in an issue, not in an `output` fence\n//! ([kantord/toylang#129](https://github.com/kantord/toylang/issues/129)).\n//!\n//! `toylang slow` is the one deliberate exception, and it is not a skip. A `slow` fragment is\n//! an `output` fragment that the default run only type-checks and emits on every backend -- it\n//! still rots the moment the language stops accepting it -- and executes on every backend only\n//! when asked to, `TOYLANG_SLOW=1 just test` (or the `just slow-test` recipe). The tier is for\n//! programs that are verified but take the interpreted backends minutes to run, currently Euler\n//! 10 and 14 ([kantord/toylang#90](https://github.com/kantord/toylang/issues/90)); it is for\n//! measured cost, not impatience, so a fragment only earns it by being slow in fact.\n\nmod support;\n\nuse std::path::{Path, PathBuf};\n\nuse support::Expect;\n\n/// What a fragment claims happens to its program.\nenum Outcome {\n    /// Compiles, runs on every backend, and they all print this.\n    Output(String),\n    /// Compiles, and every backend refuses to run it. The fence is empty: what each backend\n    /// says while refusing is its own business, so there is no text to pin.\n    Refusal,\n    /// Does not compile, and this is the checker's message, verbatim.\n    Error(String),\n}\n\nstruct Fragment {\n    /// `page:line` of the opening fence, so a failure points at the prose to fix.\n    at: String,\n    program: String,\n    input: Option<String>,\n    outcome: Outcome,\n    /// A `toylang slow` fence: type-checked and emitted on every backend in the default run,\n    /// executed on every backend only under `TOYLANG_SLOW=1`.\n    slow: bool,\n}\n\n/// A `toylang` fence whose expectation fence has not arrived yet.\nstruct Pending {\n    at: String,\n    program: String,\n    input: Option<String>,\n    slow: bool,\n}\n\n/// Fills in a pending fragment's stdin, from an `input` fence: refuses a second one, and a\n/// fence with no fragment open to receive it.\nfn set_pending_input(pending: &mut Option<Pending>, at: &str, body: String) {\n    match pending {\n        Some(p) if p.input.is_none() => p.input = Some(body),\n        Some(Pending { at: prev, .. }) => panic!(\"{at}: fragment at {prev} already has an input\"),\n        None => panic!(\"{at}: `input` fence with no `toylang` fence before it\"),\n    }\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn every_fragment_is_a_real_program() {\n    let mut fragments = Vec::new();\n    let mut embedded = Vec::new();\n    for (page, text) in pages() {\n        extract(&page, &text, &mut fragments, &mut embedded);\n    }\n    assert!(\n        !fragments.is_empty(),\n        \"the docs have no fragments, so this test proves nothing\"\n    );\n    assert!(\n        fragments.iter().any(|f| f.slow),\n        \"no docs fragment is marked `slow`, so the tier proves nothing; delete it\"\n    );\n\n    let mut failures = Vec::new();\n\n    for f in &fragments {\n        if f.slow && !slow_runs_enabled() {\n            failures.extend(compile_only_failures(f));\n            continue;\n        }\n        match &f.outcome {\n            Outcome::Output(want) => failures.extend(support::agreement_failures(\n                &f.at,\n                &f.program,\n                f.input.as_deref(),\n                &Expect::Output(want.clone()),\n            )),\n            Outcome::Refusal => {\n                // Compiling is checked first and separately: to `agreement_failures` a program\n                // that does not compile looks exactly like one every backend refused.\n                if let Err(e) = toylang::compile(&f.program) {\n                    failures.push(format!(\"BROKEN  {}: does not compile: {e}\", f.at));\n                } else {\n                    failures.extend(support::agreement_failures(\n                        &f.at,\n                        &f.program,\n                        f.input.as_deref(),\n                        &Expect::Refusal,\n                    ));\n                }\n            }\n            Outcome::Error(want) => match toylang::compile(&f.program) {\n                Ok(_) => failures.push(format!(\n                    \"COMPILED {}: claims `{want}` but the program compiles\",\n                    f.at\n                )),\n                Err(e) if e.to_string() != *want => failures.push(format!(\n                    \"MISQUOTED {}: the checker says {:?}, the page says {want:?}\",\n                    f.at,\n                    e.to_string()\n                )),\n                Err(_) => {}\n            },\n        }\n    }\n\n    for (at, id) in &embedded {\n        if !support::dir().join(format!(\"{id}.yaml\")).is_file() {\n            failures.push(format!(\n                \"MISSING {at}: embeds corpus case `{id}`, which does not exist\"\n            ));\n        }\n    }\n\n    assert!(\n        failures.is_empty(),\n        \"{} of {} docs fragments failed:\\n{}\",\n        failures.len(),\n        fragments.len(),\n        failures.join(\"\\n\")\n    );\n}\n\n/// Whether the slow tier executes. `TOYLANG_SLOW=1` turns it on; the default run compiles a\n/// `slow` fragment on every backend without running it.\nfn slow_runs_enabled() -> bool {\n    std::env::var(\"TOYLANG_SLOW\").is_ok_and(|v| v == \"1\")\n}\n\n/// The default check for a `slow` fragment. Running it is what costs minutes, so the default\n/// run proves only what is cheap: it parses, type-checks, and emits on every backend. The\n/// agreement and the printed answer are the opt-in run's claim.\nfn compile_only_failures(f: &Fragment) -> Vec<String> {\n    let program = match toylang::compile(&f.program) {\n        Ok(p) => p,\n        Err(e) => return vec![format!(\"BROKEN  {}: does not compile: {e}\", f.at)],\n    };\n    let mut failures = Vec::new();\n    for backend in toylang::Backend::ALL {\n        if let Err(e) = backend.emit(&program) {\n            failures.push(format!(\n                \"BROKEN  {}: {} could not emit: {e}\",\n                f.at,\n                backend.name()\n            ));\n        }\n    }\n    failures\n}\n\n/// The reference is complete for everything implemented, checkably: a builtin without a page\n/// fails here, the same way a corpus case without fresh tags fails tag_corpus.\n#[test]\nfn every_builtin_has_a_reference_page() {\n    let missing: Vec<&str> = toylang::check::BUILTIN_NAMES\n        .into_iter()\n        .filter(|name| {\n            !docs_dir()\n                .join(\"reference/builtins\")\n                .join(format!(\"{name}.md\"))\n                .is_file()\n        })\n        .collect();\n    assert!(\n        missing.is_empty(),\n        \"builtins without a reference page under docs/reference/builtins/: {}\",\n        missing.join(\", \")\n    );\n}\n\nfn repo_root() -> PathBuf {\n    Path::new(env!(\"CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR\")).to_path_buf()\n}\n\nfn docs_dir() -> PathBuf {\n    repo_root().join(\"docs\")\n}\n\n/// Every markdown page under `docs/`, as (path relative to the repo, content), sorted so\n/// failures come out in a stable order.\nfn pages() -> Vec<(String, String)> {\n    let mut paths = Vec::new();\n    walk(&docs_dir(), &mut paths);\n    paths.sort();\n    paths\n        .into_iter()\n        .map(|p| {\n            let text =\n                std::fs::read_to_string(&p).unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!(\"{}: {e}\", p.display()));\n            let rel = p\n                .strip_prefix(Path::new(env!(\"CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR\")))\n                .expect(\"under the repo\")\n                .to_string_lossy()\n                .into_owned();\n            (rel, text)\n        })\n        .collect()\n}\n\nfn walk(dir: &Path, out: &mut Vec<PathBuf>) {\n    for entry in\n        std::fs::read_dir(dir).unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!(\"cannot read {}: {e}\", dir.display()))\n    {\n        let path = entry.expect(\"readable entry\").path();\n        if path.is_dir() {\n            walk(&path, out);\n        } else if path.extension().is_some_and(|e| e == \"md\") {\n            out.push(path);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n/// Pulls the fragments out of one page. Malformed structure -- an `input` with no program, a\n/// program left without an expectation -- is a panic naming the line, like a malformed corpus\n/// case: a fence that asks for nothing looks exactly like a fence that passes.\nfn extract(\n    page: &str,\n    text: &str,\n    fragments: &mut Vec<Fragment>,\n    embedded: &mut Vec<(String, String)>,\n) {\n    // A `toylang` fence whose expectation fence has not arrived yet.\n    let mut pending: Option<Pending> = None;\n    let mut lines = text.lines().enumerate().peekable();\n\n    while let Some((i, line)) = lines.next() {\n        let Some(info) = line.strip_prefix(\"```\") else {\n            continue;\n        };\n        let at = format!(\"{page}:{}\", i + 1);\n        let mut body = String::new();\n        loop {\n            match lines.next() {\n                Some((_, \"```\")) => break,\n                Some((_, l)) => {\n                    body.push_str(l);\n                    body.push('\\n');\n                }\n                None => panic!(\"{at}: fence never closes\"),\n            }\n        }\n        match info.trim() {\n            \"toylang\" | \"toylang slow\" => {\n                if let Some(Pending { at: prev, .. }) = &pending {\n                    panic!(\"{prev}: fragment has no `output`, `refuses`, or `error` fence\");\n                }\n                pending = Some(Pending {\n                    at,\n                    program: body,\n                    input: None,\n                    slow: info.trim() == \"toylang slow\",\n                });\n            }\n            \"input\" => set_pending_input(&mut pending, &at, body),\n            \"output\" | \"refuses\" | \"error\" => {\n                let Some(p) = pending.take() else {\n                    panic!(\n                        \"{at}: `{}` fence with no `toylang` fence before it\",\n                        info.trim()\n                    )\n                };\n                assert!(\n                    !p.slow || info.trim() == \"output\",\n                    \"{at}: a `slow` fragment runs on every backend and agrees, so it needs an \\\n                     `output` fence\"\n                );\n                let outcome = match info.trim() {\n                    \"output\" => Outcome::Output(body),\n                    \"error\" => Outcome::Error(body.trim_end().to_string()),\n                    _ => {\n                        assert!(\n                            body.is_empty(),\n                            \"{at}: a `refuses` fence is empty; what each backend says while \\\n                             refusing is its own business\"\n                        );\n                        Outcome::Refusal\n                    }\n                };\n                fragments.push(Fragment {\n                    at: p.at,\n                    program: p.program,\n                    input: p.input,\n                    outcome,\n                    slow: p.slow,\n                });\n            }\n            \"case\" => {\n                let id = body.trim();\n                assert!(\n                    !id.is_empty() && !id.contains(char::is_whitespace),\n                    \"{at}: a `case` fence holds one corpus case id\"\n                );\n                embedded.push((at, id.to_string()));\n            }\n            // Any other language is ordinary illustration, not a claim about the compiler.\n            _ => {}\n        }\n    }\n    if let Some(Pending { at: prev, .. }) = pending {\n        panic!(\"{prev}: fragment has no `output`, `refuses`, or `error` fence\");\n    }\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":328,"totalLines":328,"truncated":false}},"title":"tests/docs.rs","time":{"start":1788292033617,"end":1788292033628}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I understand the tests. Let me look at the docs.rs test to understand how docs fragments are validated, and the step tests. Let me also look at the corpus.rs test to understand how corpus cases are run.\n\nLet me look at the tests/docs.rs and tests/corpus.rs.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05e82ebae001L1nMwEVE1FqI5i","sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","messageID":"msg_05e82e621001zD6KxqzUNLmvpB"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1788292033660,"sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","part":{"id":"prt_05e82ec77001OKo5AHZBY4lCx6","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"0ebf070b795b2e106627c5d8e519929a4e05dbe7","messageID":"msg_05e82e621001zD6KxqzUNLmvpB","sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":88774,"input":1524,"output":65,"reasoning":59,"cache":{"write":0,"read":87126}},"cost":0.001515396}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788292034757,"sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","part":{"id":"prt_05e82f0bb001y4KJpQ2o9sbrsS","messageID":"msg_05e82ec91001mW1NqAwADrcBYF","sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","snapshot":"0ebf070b795b2e106627c5d8e519929a4e05dbe7","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788292035092,"sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_01a05e82f1037e23abf2efa6","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/tests/corpus.rs"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/tests/corpus.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: //! The agreement harness.\n2: //!\n3: //! One corpus, every backend. This is the check a single-backend suite cannot express: two\n4: //! backends producing different answers is a failure in its own right, separate from either of\n5: //! them producing a wrong one. The step_*.rs suites keep their own job, which is pinning error\n6: //! messages and emitted code; this is about behaviour, and behaviour is what has to be identical\n7: //! across targets.\n8: \n9: mod support;\n10: \n11: /// The agreement check for one slice of the corpus: every `of`th case by index. Splitting by\n12: /// index, not by backend, is the point -- `agreement_failures` runs every backend internally, so\n13: /// the case is the unit of work a parallel runner can hand to a thread, and each shard keeps the\n14: /// same bar a single test always had.\n15: fn assert_shard_agrees(shard: usize, of: usize) {\n16:     let cases = support::cases();\n17:     assert!(\n18:         !cases.is_empty(),\n19:         \"the corpus is empty, so this test proves nothing\"\n20:     );\n21: \n22:     let mut failures = Vec::new();\n23: \n24:     for (i, case) in cases.iter().enumerate() {\n25:         if i % of != shard {\n26:             continue;\n27:         }\n28:         failures.extend(support::agreement_failures(\n29:             &case.name,\n30:             &case.program,\n31:             case.input.as_deref(),\n32:             &case.expect,\n33:         ));\n34:     }\n35: \n36:     assert!(\n37:         failures.is_empty(),\n38:         \"{} corpus programs failed in shard {shard}/{of}:\\n{}\",\n39:         failures.len(),\n40:         failures.join(\"\\n\")\n41:     );\n42: }\n43: \n44: #[test]\n45: fn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_0() {\n46:     assert_shard_agrees(0, 8);\n47: }\n48: #[test]\n49: fn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_1() {\n50:     assert_shard_agrees(1, 8);\n51: }\n52: #[test]\n53: fn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_2() {\n54:     assert_shard_agrees(2, 8);\n55: }\n56: #[test]\n57: fn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_3() {\n58:     assert_shard_agrees(3, 8);\n59: }\n60: #[test]\n61: fn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_4() {\n62:     assert_shard_agrees(4, 8);\n63: }\n64: #[test]\n65: fn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_5() {\n66:     assert_shard_agrees(5, 8);\n67: }\n68: #[test]\n69: fn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_6() {\n70:     assert_shard_agrees(6, 8);\n71: }\n72: #[test]\n73: fn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_7() {\n74:     assert_shard_agrees(7, 8);\n75: }\n76: \n77: /// What a program prints is not always the whole claim. A case that asks for it gets the code a\n78: /// backend emitted pinned as well, which is how a property invisible in the output -- a name\n79: /// that has to be declared, a type that has to be spelled -- stays observed.\n80: #[test]\n81: fn emitted_code_matches_the_snapshot() {\n82:     let mut asked = 0;\n83:     for case in support::cases() {\n84:         let program =\n85:             toylang::compile(&case.program).unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!(\"{}: {e}\", case.name));\n86:         for backend in case.snapshot {\n87:             let emitted = backend.emit(&program).unwrap_or_else(|e| {\n88:                 panic!(\"{}: {} could not emit: {e}\", case.name, backend.name())\n89:             });\n90:             insta::assert_snapshot!(format!(\"{}__{}\", case.name, backend.name()), emitted);\n91:             asked += 1;\n92:         }\n93:     }\n94:     assert!(\n95:         asked > 0,\n96:         \"no case asks for a snapshot, so this test proves nothing\"\n97:     );\n98: }\n\n(End of file - total 98 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"//! The agreement harness.\n//!\n//! One corpus, every backend. This is the check a single-backend suite cannot express: two\n//! backends producing different answers is a failure in its own right, separate from either of\n//! them producing a wrong one. The step_*.rs suites keep their own job, which is pinning error\n//! messages and emitted code; this is about behaviour, and behaviour is what has to be identical\n//! across targets.\n\nmod support;\n\n/// The agreement check for one slice of the corpus: every `of`th case by index. Splitting by\n/// index, not by backend, is the point -- `agreement_failures` runs every backend internally, so\n/// the case is the unit of work a parallel runner can hand to a thread, and each shard keeps the\n/// same bar a single test always had.\nfn assert_shard_agrees(shard: usize, of: usize) {\n    let cases = support::cases();\n    assert!(\n        !cases.is_empty(),\n        \"the corpus is empty, so this test proves nothing\"\n    );","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/tests/corpus.rs","text":"//! The agreement harness.\n//!\n//! One corpus, every backend. This is the check a single-backend suite cannot express: two\n//! backends producing different answers is a failure in its own right, separate from either of\n//! them producing a wrong one. The step_*.rs suites keep their own job, which is pinning error\n//! messages and emitted code; this is about behaviour, and behaviour is what has to be identical\n//! across targets.\n\nmod support;\n\n/// The agreement check for one slice of the corpus: every `of`th case by index. Splitting by\n/// index, not by backend, is the point -- `agreement_failures` runs every backend internally, so\n/// the case is the unit of work a parallel runner can hand to a thread, and each shard keeps the\n/// same bar a single test always had.\nfn assert_shard_agrees(shard: usize, of: usize) {\n    let cases = support::cases();\n    assert!(\n        !cases.is_empty(),\n        \"the corpus is empty, so this test proves nothing\"\n    );\n\n    let mut failures = Vec::new();\n\n    for (i, case) in cases.iter().enumerate() {\n        if i % of != shard {\n            continue;\n        }\n        failures.extend(support::agreement_failures(\n            &case.name,\n            &case.program,\n            case.input.as_deref(),\n            &case.expect,\n        ));\n    }\n\n    assert!(\n        failures.is_empty(),\n        \"{} corpus programs failed in shard {shard}/{of}:\\n{}\",\n        failures.len(),\n        failures.join(\"\\n\")\n    );\n}\n\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_0() {\n    assert_shard_agrees(0, 8);\n}\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_1() {\n    assert_shard_agrees(1, 8);\n}\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_2() {\n    assert_shard_agrees(2, 8);\n}\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_3() {\n    assert_shard_agrees(3, 8);\n}\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_4() {\n    assert_shard_agrees(4, 8);\n}\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_5() {\n    assert_shard_agrees(5, 8);\n}\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_6() {\n    assert_shard_agrees(6, 8);\n}\n#[test]\nfn every_backend_agrees_and_is_right_shard_7() {\n    assert_shard_agrees(7, 8);\n}\n\n/// What a program prints is not always the whole claim. A case that asks for it gets the code a\n/// backend emitted pinned as well, which is how a property invisible in the output -- a name\n/// that has to be declared, a type that has to be spelled -- stays observed.\n#[test]\nfn emitted_code_matches_the_snapshot() {\n    let mut asked = 0;\n    for case in support::cases() {\n        let program =\n            toylang::compile(&case.program).unwrap_or_else(|e| panic!(\"{}: {e}\", case.name));\n        for backend in case.snapshot {\n            let emitted = backend.emit(&program).unwrap_or_else(|e| {\n                panic!(\"{}: {} could not emit: {e}\", case.name, backend.name())\n            });\n            insta::assert_snapshot!(format!(\"{}__{}\", case.name, backend.name()), emitted);\n            asked += 1;\n        }\n    }\n    assert!(\n        asked > 0,\n        \"no case asks for a snapshot, so this test proves nothing\"\n    );\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":98,"totalLines":98,"truncated":false}},"title":"tests/corpus.rs","time":{"start":1788292035080,"end":1788292035090}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now let me look at the corpus.rs test and the step tests to understand the test structure. Let me also look at the tests/corpus.rs.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05e82f185001OFvUgI0iVvaKF0","sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","messageID":"msg_05e82ec91001mW1NqAwADrcBYF"}}
