{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788292109770,"sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-bf404da5646ce5f0","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/tests/corpus/input_typed_by_first_use.yaml"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/tests/corpus/input_typed_by_first_use.yaml</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Goal program three of the type-flow rework (plans/type-flow.md), verbatim from the\n2: # oddities inventory: the first `input` is typed by `f`'s parameter, and the second use --\n3: # a record field nothing expects anything of -- borrows the type the first use fixed.\n4: program: |\n5:   fn f(v: Vec<Int>) -> Int = length(v)\n6: \n7:   {a: f(input), b: input}\n8: \n9: input: |\n10:   [1, 2, 3]\n11: \n12: output: |\n13:   {\"a\":3,\"b\":[1,2,3]}\n14: \n15: node_types: [application, builtin.length, input, record-literal, var]\n\n(End of file - total 15 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Goal program three of the type-flow rework (plans/type-flow.md), verbatim from the\n# oddities inventory: the first `input` is typed by `f`'s parameter, and the second use --\n# a record field nothing expects anything of -- borrows the type the first use fixed.\nprogram: |\n  fn f(v: Vec<Int>) -> Int = length(v)\n\n  {a: f(input), b: input}\n\ninput: |\n  [1, 2, 3]\n\noutput: |\n  {\"a\":3,\"b\":[1,2,3]}\n\nnode_types: [application, builtin.length, input, record-literal, var]","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/tests/corpus/input_typed_by_first_use.yaml","text":"# Goal program three of the type-flow rework (plans/type-flow.md), verbatim from the\n# oddities inventory: the first `input` is typed by `f`'s parameter, and the second use --\n# a record field nothing expects anything of -- borrows the type the first use fixed.\nprogram: |\n  fn f(v: Vec<Int>) -> Int = length(v)\n\n  {a: f(input), b: input}\n\ninput: |\n  [1, 2, 3]\n\noutput: |\n  {\"a\":3,\"b\":[1,2,3]}\n\nnode_types: [application, builtin.length, input, record-literal, var]","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":15,"totalLines":15,"truncated":false}},"title":"tests/corpus/input_typed_by_first_use.yaml","time":{"start":1788292109759,"end":1788292109769}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I have a good understanding. Let me now plan the implementation.\n\n## Design\n\nThe ruling: add `;` after every `fn` and `type` declaration. Retire the same-line rule and its diagnostic.\n\nLet me think about the whole set of changes:\n\n### 1. Lexer: add `;` token\nAdd `Tok::Semicolon` and map `;` → `Tok::Semicolon` in `read_tok`.\n\n### 2. Parser changes:\n\n**`def()`**: After reading the body, require `;`. Change `def` to end with `eat(Tok::Semicolon)`.\n\nWait, but `def_body` reads an expression. Then after the body, we need to eat the `;`. Let me look at how `def` returns. It returns `Def` with `span: start.to(body.span())`. I'll add `self.eat(Tok::Semicolon)?` after `let body = self.def_body()?;`.\n\n**`alias()`**: `type X = ...` — after reading the type, require `;`. Change to `let ty = self.type_expr()?; self.eat(Tok::Semicolon)?;`.\n\n**`enum_decl()`**: The ruling says \"after every `fn` and `type` declaration\". Enums end with `}` which is self-delimiting. But wait — let me reconsider. Does the enum need a terminator? Consider the defs loop:\n```\nloop {\n    match tok {\n        Tok::Pub => ...,\n        Tok::Fn => defs.push(p.def(false)?),\n        Tok::Type => aliases.push(p.alias()?),\n        Tok::Enum => enums.push(p.enum_decl(false)?),\n        _ => break,\n    }\n}\n```\nAfter `enum_decl`, the next token is whatever follows `}`. If enum doesn't get a `;`, then the loop continues and reads the next declaration. That works fine since `}` delimits the enum. The issue explicitly says \"after every `fn` and `type` declaration\". So enums don't need a terminator. But wait — the enum's `}` boundary... hmm. Actually let me reconsider whether enums should also get a terminator for consistency.\n\nThe issue says \"require it after every `fn` and `type` declaration\". So enums are excluded. I'll follow the issue literally. Enums keep their `}`.\n\nHmm, but actually — let me reconsider. The `enum` keyword: does the enum body `}` create an ambiguity with the program body? No, because `}` is self-delimiting. The program body follows after `}`. So no terminator needed for enums.\n\nBut wait, there's a subtlety. What about `parse_module`? It also reads `fn`/`type`/`enum`. Let me handle both.\n\nActually wait — let me reconsider the scope. The `parse_module` function reads module declarations. Modules are `prelude.toy`'s format (declarations only). So `prelude.toy`'s declarations would need `;` terminators too. Let me check prelude.toy — it has `pub enum Opt<T>`, `pub enum Result<T, E>`, and several `pub fn`. If we require `;` after fn/type, then prelude.toy's fns need `;`.\n\nActually wait — does `parse_module` even handle `type`? Let me re-read:\n```rust\npub fn parse_module(src: &str) -> Result<Module, Error> {\n    ...\n    loop {\n        let (tok, span) = p.peek()?;\n        match tok {\n            Tok::Pub => { ... },\n            Tok::Fn => defs.push(p.def(false)?),\n            Tok::Enum => enums.push(p.enum_decl(false)?),\n            Tok::Eof => break,\n            other => return Err(...),\n        }\n    }\n    Ok(Module { defs, enums })\n}\n```\nModules don't handle `type` (aliases). So modules only have fn and enum. Fns need `;`. So prelude.toy's fns need `;`.\n\nHmm, but modules don't have a program body. So after the last `fn ...;`, the parser hits Eof. Good.\n\n### 3. Retire the same-line rule\n\nNow the tricky part. The same-line rule is used in:\n1. `takes_argument` in `ident_expr` (call arguments) and variant payloads.\n2. The `postfix` `[` indexing rule.\n3. The `input <type>` declaration.\n4. The `def_body` let-binding line rule (this is a different rule, \"a `let` binding must fit on its line\" — this is the let-sequence form, NOT the same-line rule). Actually let me check: `def_body` uses `self.same_line(binding_start, value.span().end)` for the let-block. That's a separate rule about let bindings fitting on their line. The brief says retire \"the same-line heuristic\" — I think that's specifically the call-argument/declaration-boundary same-line rule, not the let-binding line rule. Let me keep the let rule.\n\nWait, but if I remove the `same_line` method, the let rule uses it. Let me look at how the let rule works. It checks `self.same_line(binding_start, value.span().end)` — \"a `let` binding must fit on its line\". This is a genuinely different rule (let blocks are line-structured). So I should keep this. But it uses `same_line`. \n\nHmm. The brief says \"retiring the same-line heuristic (parser + checker + error message)\". Let me be careful about what exactly to retire.\n\nLet me re-read the issue's intent: \"retire the same-line rule and its diagnostic once the terminator makes the ambiguity unreachable.\" The ambiguity is the definition-body/program-body boundary. The same-line rule was introduced for call arguments to keep the boundary safe. Once the terminator handles the boundary, the same-line rule for call arguments is no longer needed.\n\nBut the let-binding rule is a separate design decision (issue #87) about line-structured let blocks. That should stay.\n\nSo I need to:\n- Remove the same-line requirement for call arguments (`takes_argument`), and remove `declined_cross_line` + its diagnostic.\n- Remove the same-line requirement for `[` indexing.\n- Handle the `input <type>` declaration disambiguation.\n- Keep the let-binding line rule (but it uses `same_line`, so I need to keep `same_line` or inline it).\n\nActually, let me reconsider the `input` case. Let me look at what happens with `input` more carefully.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether retiring the same-line rule for call arguments is safe given the `input` declaration. Let me think about the whole picture.\n\nWith the terminator, the grammar is:\n```\nprogram := (fn ...; | type ...; | enum ...)* [input <type>] expr\n```\n\nWait, but does `input <type>` get a terminator? The ruling only mentions fn and type. So `input <type>` doesn't get a `;`. But it's read before the body. How does the parser know where `input <type>` ends and the body begins? The type is self-delimiting (record type ends with `}`, named type is a single name, Vec<...> ends with `>`). So it works.\n\nBut the disambiguation: `input` followed by `{` or `Ident` → declaration. If I remove the same-line check, then `input` followed by `{` or `Ident` on any line is a declaration.\n\nNow, is there a real program where `input` (as a value, the program body) is followed by `{` or `Ident`? Let me think:\n- `input` as the program body, followed by nothing: `input` alone. Fine.\n- `input` as program body followed by a record literal: `input\\n{a: 1}`. This is currently a parse error (two expressions in a row). After removing same-line, `input {a: 1}` would be parsed as `input` declared as type `{a: 1}`. Since `1` is not a type, this fails as a type parse error. Hmm, slightly different error but still an error.\n\nActually wait, let me reconsider. What about `input` followed by a call on the next line in a pipeline context? Like:\n```\ninput\n| map(...)\n```\nNo wait, `input` is a value, and `| map(...)` would be a pipe. But the `input <type>` declaration check happens before the body parse. If `input` is followed by `|`, that's not `{` or `Ident`, so it's not a declaration — it's the body. Good.\n\nHmm, what about `input` followed by an identifier that's a function call? Like `input\\nf(x)`? Currently: `input` value, then `f(x)` — parse error (two expressions). After removing same-line: `input f(x)`... wait, `input` followed by `f` (an Ident) would be parsed as `input <type>` where type = `f(x)`? No, `f` is a named type `f`, then `(x)` — hmm, after `type_expr` reads `f`, the parser is done with the type. Then the body starts at `(x)`. Wait no. Let me re-trace.\n\nActually `input <type>` — `type_expr` reads `f` as a named type. Then `input` declaration is done. Then `let body = p.expr(0)?` reads `(x)`. Hmm, that would be a body `(x)` = `x`. That's weird but... would that be a valid program? `input` declared as type `f` (a function name, which isn't a valid type). This would fail in the checker. So it's an error either way.\n\nOK so the `input` disambiguation is not really the concern of the same-line rule in a way that matters for valid programs. But let me think about whether removing the same-line check could break a valid program.\n\nConsider: a program that uses `input` as a value where input is the program body, and the program body is `input`. That's just `input` alone. Fine.\n\nConsider a program where `input` appears as a value somewhere, and also the file has an `input <type>` declaration. E.g.:\n```\nfn f(x: Int) -> Int = x\n\ninput {x: Int}\nf(input)\n```\nHere `input` is both a declaration (`input {x: Int}`) and a value (`f(input)`). The declaration `input {x: Int}` — `input` followed by `{x: Int}` on the same line. The value `f(input)` — `input` inside parens, followed by `)`. So when parsing the body `f(input)`, `input` is read as a value (atom), not a declaration, because the declaration check only happens at the top of the program before the body.\n\nSo the `input` declaration disambiguation only matters at the top of the program, right after the defs. And there, if `input` is followed by `{` or `Ident`, it's a declaration. If I remove the same-line check, then `input` at the top followed by `{` or `Ident` on the next line would become a declaration.\n\nIs there a valid program where the top-level expression starts with `input` followed by `{` or `Ident` on the next line? \n- Top-level expr `input` then `{...}` — invalid (two exprs).\n- Top-level expr `input` then `ident(...)` — invalid (two exprs).\n\nSo removing the same-line check for `input` doesn't break any valid program. The only change is error messages for invalid programs. That's acceptable — the ruling says retire the same-line heuristic.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider. There might be a subtle case. What if the top-level expression is a pipeline or something starting with `input`? Like:\n```\ninput\n| map(...)\n```\n`input` followed by `|`. Not `{` or `Ident`. So it's treated as the body (value). Good, no change.\n\nWhat about `input` as the start of a call argument or something at the top level? The top-level is just one expression. The only way `input` is followed directly by `{` or `Ident` at the very top of the program is the two-expressions-in-a-row case, which is invalid.\n\nHmm, wait, actually there's another subtle case. What if `input` is used as a value and it's the last def-like thing... no. Let me just go with removing the same-line check for `input`, treating `input` followed by `{` or `Ident` as a declaration. But actually, let me reconsider — maybe I should keep the `input` disambiguation using the same-line rule but... no. The brief says retire the same-line heuristic. Let me just make `input` followed by `{` or `Ident` (any line) a declaration.\n\nWait, but actually there's a real concern. Consider the corpus case `input_typed_by_first_use`:\n```\ninput\nf(input)\n```\nLet me look at it.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05e84117c00167wVUR3TsNtxyl","sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","messageID":"msg_05e83023a00110VW2J7Rzw3W5r"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788292110338,"sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-80a3645e36a30b0b","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/tests/corpus/type_alias.yaml"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/tests/corpus/type_alias.yaml</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # An alias is an abbreviation, so this program is the same program as one with the shapes\n2: # written out, and every backend emits the same bytes for both.\n3: program: |\n4:   type User = {name: Str, age: Int}\n5:   type Db = {users: Vec<User>}\n6: \n7:   fn adults(db: Db) -> Vec<Str> = db.users | select(.age >= 18) | .[].name\n8: \n9:   adults input\n10: \n11: input: |\n12:   {\"users\": [{\"name\": \"ada\", \"age\": 36}, {\"name\": \"bo\", \"age\": 9}]}\n13: \n14: output: |\n15:   [\"ada\"]\n16: \n17: node_types: [application, compare.ge, input, int, local, pipe, projection, selection.narrow, var]\n\n(End of file - total 17 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# An alias is an abbreviation, so this program is the same program as one with the shapes\n# written out, and every backend emits the same bytes for both.\nprogram: |\n  type User = {name: Str, age: Int}\n  type Db = {users: Vec<User>}\n\n  fn adults(db: Db) -> Vec<Str> = db.users | select(.age >= 18) | .[].name\n\n  adults input\n\ninput: |\n  {\"users\": [{\"name\": \"ada\", \"age\": 36}, {\"name\": \"bo\", \"age\": 9}]}\n\noutput: |\n  [\"ada\"]\n\nnode_types: [application, compare.ge, input, int, local, pipe, projection, selection.narrow, var]","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/tests/corpus/type_alias.yaml","text":"# An alias is an abbreviation, so this program is the same program as one with the shapes\n# written out, and every backend emits the same bytes for both.\nprogram: |\n  type User = {name: Str, age: Int}\n  type Db = {users: Vec<User>}\n\n  fn adults(db: Db) -> Vec<Str> = db.users | select(.age >= 18) | .[].name\n\n  adults input\n\ninput: |\n  {\"users\": [{\"name\": \"ada\", \"age\": 36}, {\"name\": \"bo\", \"age\": 9}]}\n\noutput: |\n  [\"ada\"]\n\nnode_types: [application, compare.ge, input, int, local, pipe, projection, selection.narrow, var]","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":17,"totalLines":17,"truncated":false}},"title":"tests/corpus/type_alias.yaml","time":{"start":1788292110330,"end":1788292110335}},"id":"prt_05e8415c40014zR1oPlW6el9jT","sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","messageID":"msg_05e83023a00110VW2J7Rzw3W5r"}}
