{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788292191282,"sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8d81232344d2cf69","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/tests/corpus/vec_body_after_definition.yaml"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/tests/corpus/vec_body_after_definition.yaml</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # The vec-literal program body used to be swallowed across the definition boundary as an\n2: # index on the definition's trailing expression (issue #148). An index must start on the same\n3: # line as its base, and this `[` does not.\n4: program: |\n5:   fn user_fn(x: Int) -> Int = x * 2\n6: \n7:   [user_fn(1), user_fn(2)]\n8:  \n9: output: |\n10:   [2,4]\n11: \n12: node_types: [application, arith.mul, int, var, vec-literal]\n\n(End of file - total 12 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# The vec-literal program body used to be swallowed across the definition boundary as an\n# index on the definition's trailing expression (issue #148). An index must start on the same\n# line as its base, and this `[` does not.\nprogram: |\n  fn user_fn(x: Int) -> Int = x * 2\n\n  [user_fn(1), user_fn(2)]\n \noutput: |\n  [2,4]\n\nnode_types: [application, arith.mul, int, var, vec-literal]","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/tests/corpus/vec_body_after_definition.yaml","text":"# The vec-literal program body used to be swallowed across the definition boundary as an\n# index on the definition's trailing expression (issue #148). An index must start on the same\n# line as its base, and this `[` does not.\nprogram: |\n  fn user_fn(x: Int) -> Int = x * 2\n\n  [user_fn(1), user_fn(2)]\n \noutput: |\n  [2,4]\n\nnode_types: [application, arith.mul, int, var, vec-literal]","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":12,"totalLines":12,"truncated":false}},"title":"tests/corpus/vec_body_after_definition.yaml","time":{"start":1788292191275,"end":1788292191281}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Interesting! `type_alias.yaml` has:\n```\nfn adults(db: Db) -> Vec<Str> = db.users | select(.age >= 18) | .[].name\n\nadults input\n```\nHere the body is `adults input` — a bare call. `adults` is a function, `input` is the bare argument. With the same-line rule, `adults input` on the same line is fine.\n\nNow `input_typed_by_first_use.yaml`:\n```\nfn f(v: Vec<Int>) -> Int = length(v)\n\n{a: f(input), b: input}\n```\nThe body is `{a: f(input), b: input}` — starts with `{`. Not `input`. Fine.\n\nOK so the `input` disambiguation: with the terminator, after `fn f(...) = length(v);`, the parser breaks out of the defs loop (next token is `{`). Then the input check: `input` not next, so no declaration. Then body = `{a: f(input), b: input}`. Good.\n\nNow the `adults input` case: after `fn adults(...) = ...;`, the defs loop sees `adults` (an Ident), which breaks the loop. Then the input check: `input` is not the next token (`adults` is). So no input declaration. Body = `adults input`. \n\nNow, consider what happens if I retire the same-line rule and the input same-line check. Let me think about the case where the program body starts with `input` as a value:\n```\nfn f(v: Vec<Int>) -> Int = length(v)\n\ninput\n```\nHere after the defs loop (`;` terminated), the next token is `input`. The input check: `input` followed by... next token is `Eof`. `Eof` is not `{` or `Ident`. So no declaration. Body = `input`. Good.\n\nAnd:\n```\nfn f(v: Vec<Int>) -> Int = length(v)\n\ninput f\n```\nHmm, `input` followed by `f` (an Ident) — that would be treated as a declaration `input <type f>`. But `f` is a function name, not a type. This would fail in the checker (\"expected a type...\" no wait, `type_expr` reads `f` as a named type fine). Then body is empty → parse error \"expected end of program...\". Wait no. Let me re-trace. If `input f` is a declaration, then after reading type `f`, we're done with declarations. Then `let body = p.expr(0)?` — but we're at Eof. `p.expr(0)` would fail with \"expected an expression, found end of program\". \n\nBut this program (`input` followed by `f` on the next line) — is it a valid program currently? `input` as a value, then `f` on the next line — two expressions in a row → parse error. So it's invalid either way. No valid program breaks.\n\nOK. Now let me also reconsider: what about the `input <type>` — should it also need a `;`? The issue doesn't say so. But wait — actually let me reconsider. There might be a subtle issue with `input <type>` not having a terminator. Consider:\n\n```\nfn f(x: Int) -> Int = x;\n\ninput Int\nf(input)\n```\n\nThe defs loop reads `fn f(x: Int) -> Int = x;`. Then breaks at `input`. Then the input check reads `input Int` as a declaration. Then body = `f(input)`. Good.\n\nBut what about:\n```\nfn f(x: Int) -> Int = x;\n\ninput {a: Int}\n```\nThe input declaration is `input {a: Int}`. Body would be empty → parse error. That's fine.\n\nHmm, actually wait. What if there's no `input` declaration but the body starts with `input` as a value AND the program also uses `input` in the body? Like the corpus case `input_annotation`... no, that one has an explicit declaration.\n\nOK here's another thought — what if `input` is the body and the body is just `input`? That's the `step_5__bare_input.snap` case. Let me check that.\n\nActually let me not over-analyze. Let me look at all the corpus cases with `input` and the step tests, and consider each.\n\nLet me now think about the `declined_cross_line` mechanism. It's used to give the parens-spelling hint when a cross-line argument is declined and the parse then fails at that token. If we retire the same-line rule, then cross-line arguments are allowed, so this mechanism is removed entirely.\n\nNow, an important question: with the same-line rule gone, does the terminator actually make the ambiguity unreachable in ALL cases? Let me think about the case that motivated it:\n```\nfn id(s: Str) -> Str = s\n\n{a: 1}\n```\nWith terminator: `fn id(s: Str) -> Str = s;` then `{a: 1}` is the body. Good.\n\nBut what about the case where the definition body is followed by another definition?\n```\nfn f(x: Int) -> Int = x\nfn g(x: Int) -> Int = x\n```\nCurrently: the same-line rule keeps `x` (the body of f) from swallowing `fn g(...)`. Wait, actually `fn` can't start an expression, so `x` followed by `fn`... `x` is a bare var, then `fn` is not a continuation. Hmm, actually the problem is when the body is a bare identifier and the next thing could be a call argument. Let me think:\n```\nfn f(x: Int) -> Int = x\nfn g(x: Int) -> Int = x\n```\nWithout the same-line rule and without terminator: `f`'s body is `x`. Then the parser continues reading the expression `x` and sees `fn` — `fn` is not an infix or postfix or argument. So the expression `x` ends. Then the defs loop continues... wait no. Let me re-trace the parse function.\n\nActually the defs loop reads `def` which reads the body. `def_body` calls `self.expr(0)`. `expr(0)` reads `x`. Then the expr parser checks for continuations: `fn` is not a continuation (not infix, not postfix, not `if`, not pipe). So `x` ends. Back in `def`, we now eat `;`. Wait, but currently there's no `;`. With the terminator, we eat `;`. Then the defs loop continues and reads `fn g(...)`.\n\nHmm, so actually — was the ambiguity even reachable for the fn-follows-fn case? Let me think about the actual bug. The bug was: `fn id(s: Str) -> Str = s` followed by `{a: 1}`. The body `s` is a bare identifier. After `s`, the next token is `{`. In `ident_expr` (or actually in `expr`), after reading `s` as a Var, the parser sees `{` — is `{` a continuation? `{` is not an infix operator. So the expression `s` ends... but wait, `s` was read via `ident_expr`, which checks `takes_argument`. `s` followed by `{` on the next line — `{` is in `argument_starts` (Tok::LBrace → true). But `takes_argument` returns false because `{` is on a different line. So `s` stays a Var. Good — that's the same-line rule doing its job.\n\nWithout the same-line rule: `s` followed by `{` on the next line → `takes_argument` returns true → `s` swallows `{a: 1}` as a call argument → `s({a: 1})` → the program body is gone → \"expected an expression, found end of program\" (or the body gets swallowed).\n\nSo the terminator fixes this: `s;` ends the def. After `;`, the next token is `{`, which is the program body.\n\nBut wait — what if the body itself is a call that wants a record argument on the next line? E.g.:\n```\nfn f(x: Int) -> Int = g(x)\n\nh {a: 1}\n```\nWith the terminator: `f`'s body is `g(x)`, then `;`. Then the body is `h {a: 1}`. Good.\n\nAnd:\n```\nfn f(x: Int) -> Int = g {a: 1}\n```\nHere `g {a: 1}` is the body of `f`. Then `;`. Good.\n\nSo with the terminator, call arguments CAN span lines freely, because the declaration boundary is the `;`. \n\nNow, what about a multi-line call argument inside a def body followed by the program body? E.g.:\n```\nfn f(x: Int) -> Int = g(\n    x + 1\n)\n\nf(1)\n```\nWith terminator: `f`'s body is `g(x + 1)` (spanning lines), then `;`, then body `f(1)`. \n\nSo retiring the same-line rule entirely is the right call.\n\nNow, let me also think about the indexing same-line rule. Currently:\n```\nTok::LBracket => {\n    // Indexing owns `[` on the same line only\n    if !self.same_line(e.span().end, bspan.start) {\n        return Ok(e);\n    }\n    ...\n}\n```\nThis was added for issue #148: \"Fix vec literals being swallowed as indices after a definition\". The case:\n```\nfn f(...) -> ... = ...\n\n[1, 2, 3]\n```\nThe body's trailing expression `...` claimed the `[` on the next line as an index. With the terminator, this is unreachable. So the indexing same-line rule can be retired.\n\nBut wait — does retiring the indexing same-line rule break anything? With the rule gone, `v` followed by `[` on the next line becomes an index. Is that ever a problem? Consider:\n```\nfn f(v: Vec<Int>) -> Vec<Int> = v\n[1, 2, 3]\n```\nWith terminator: `v;` ends f's body. Then `[1, 2, 3]` is the program body. Good.\n\nWithout the same-line indexing rule but WITH the terminator, this works because the `;` ends the body. So the indexing rule is safe to retire.\n\nBut what about inside a program body where someone writes:\n```\n[1, 2] \n[0]\n```\nTwo vec literals in a row — `[1,2]` followed by `[0]` on the next line. With the rule retired, `[1,2]`'s postfix loop would see `[` on the next line and treat it as an index → `[1,2][0]` which is `2`. Hmm! That changes semantics!\n\nWait, let me think. Is `[1, 2]\\n[0]` a valid program today? Without the same-line rule... currently the rule says `[` on a different line is not an index, so `[1,2]` ends, then `[0]` is... a second expression → parse error \"expected end of program, found `[`\". So currently it's an error.\n\nIf I retire the rule, `[1,2][0]` → `2`. That's a silent behavior change from error to different meaning. Is that bad? Well, `[1, 2][0]` on one line is an index. On two lines... hmm. This is the kind of thing where retiring the rule makes whitespace-insensitive token streams. Actually the whole point of the ruling is that the boundary becomes a token. The `[` indexing rule was about the definition boundary specifically. But retiring it entirely has this side effect.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me re-read the issue: \"retire the same-line rule and its diagnostic once the terminator makes the ambiguity unreachable.\" The ambiguity is the declaration boundary. The `[` same-line rule (issue #148) is a consequence of the same boundary ambiguity. With the terminator, the `[` reach across the boundary is gone.\n\nBut should I retire the `[` rule entirely, or just narrow it? Let me think about what's cleaner. The brief says \"retiring the same-line heuristic (parser + checker + error message)\". It's specifically about the same-line rule. The `[` indexing rule IS the same-line rule applied to indexing.\n\nHmm, but retiring it has the `[1, 2]\\n[0]` → `[1,2][0]` = `2` side effect. Is that acceptable? Let me think about whether that's even a reasonable program. `[1, 2]\\n[0]` — a vec literal followed by another vec literal on the next line. As a program, that's `[1,2]` then `[0]` — two expressions. That's not a valid program today. If we retire the rule, it becomes `[1,2][0]` = 2. That's... a valid program with a different meaning.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider. This is the kind of whitespace-sensitivity that the whole language has been moving away from. The ruling's whole point: \"the boundary becomes a token instead of a line-position fact.\" So making `[` on any line an index is consistent with that philosophy. A user who writes `[1, 2]\\n[0]` probably... hmm, but that's ambiguous.\n\nWait, actually, let me reconsider whether retiring the `[` rule is required. The issue says \"retire the same-line rule and its diagnostic once the terminator makes the ambiguity unreachable\". The ambiguity is the declaration boundary. The `[` rule at the declaration boundary is now unreachable. But the `[` rule ALSO applies in general (e.g., inside a body).\n\nHmm, let me look at the actual purpose again. The `[` same-line rule was introduced specifically because a definition's trailing expression could swallow a `[` on the next line (the program body's vec literal). That specific case is now handled by the terminator. So the `[` rule no longer needs to exist.\n\nBut the `[1, 2]\\n[0]` case — that's not about the declaration boundary at all. That's just two vec literals on two lines. Is there a reason to keep the `[` same-line rule for that? The `[` rule as written applies everywhere, not just at the boundary. If I keep it, then `[1,2]\\n[0]` stays an error. If I retire it, it becomes an index.\n\nLet me look at how the original #148 was fixed and what tests pin it.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05e85522b001v95QFFSiaep3IT","sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","messageID":"msg_05e84182b001M5HuIPzMfwpSkD"}}
