{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788292195107,"sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","part":{"id":"prt_05e856322001CSLJST9Kg10EHZ","messageID":"msg_05e855741001YrThuT739047Ut","sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","snapshot":"0ebf070b795b2e106627c5d8e519929a4e05dbe7","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788292195623,"sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-9bb424322605085a","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/research-log/juxtaposition-is-unsafe-at-any-undelimited-boundary.md"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/research-log/juxtaposition-is-unsafe-at-any-undelimited-boundary.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: type: Lesson\n3: calendar:\n4:   - 2026-08-26\n5:   - 2026-08-28\n6: title: Juxtaposition is unsafe at any undelimited boundary\n7: description: Adding parenless function application broke a program with no application in it, because two unrelated expressions had always sat adjacent at one spot the grammar never bothered to delimit.\n8: tags:\n9:   - parsing\n10:   - syntax\n11:   - design\n12: timestamp: 2026-08-26T00:00:00Z\n13: ---\n14: \n15: `fn f(x: Int) -> Int = x` followed by `f(1)` stopped compiling the moment bare application\n16: (`f x` meaning `f(x)`) was added, with no bare application anywhere in the source. The error was\n17: `expected an expression, found end of program`: the whole program body had been consumed as the\n18: argument to `x`, the last token of `f`'s own definition.\n19: \n20: The file grammar is `(fn ... | type ...)* body`. Every other place an expression is parsed fresh\n21: -- a pipe's right side, inside `(...)`/`[...]`/`{...}`, the program's own `body` -- is either\n22: preceded by a token that anchors it (`|`, `(`, `[`, `{`) or followed by one that bounds it (`)`,\n23: `]`, `}`, or `Eof` for `body`). A definition's own body is the one exception: nothing marks where\n24: it ends. The loop that reads definitions just keeps going while it sees `fn`/`type` and falls\n25: through to `body` when it does not, so a definition's last token sits directly next to whatever\n26: comes next with no separator at all -- another definition (safe, because `fn`/`type` cannot start\n27: a bare argument) or the program's own body (unsafe, because an identifier can).\n28: \n29: The fix is a parser flag, off for exactly the undelimited top-level chain of a definition's body\n30: and restored the instant a real delimiter is entered (parens, brackets, braces), since a closing\n31: token bounds those regardless of what sits outside them. It has to be threaded through every\n32: place `expr` is called fresh from inside such a delimiter -- five call sites in this parser --\n33: because forgetting one silently reopens the hazard for that one construct rather than failing\n34: loudly.\n35: \n36: A second instance surfaced while writing enum corpus cases, and the flag does not cover it:\n37: `fn g(s: Int) -> Int = 1` followed by a program body of `[g(1), g(2)]` fails with\n38: `expected ']', found ','`, because postfix indexing reaches across the same boundary and reads\n39: the body's Vec literal as `1[g(1), ...]`. Bare application was suspended at that boundary;\n40: `[` was not, and suspending it the same way would break legitimate indexing inside a\n41: definition's body (`v[0]!`). Nothing in the corpus had ever put a `[`-literal body directly\n42: after a definition, which is how it stayed invisible for as long as bare application's version\n43: did. And a third: the parenless record-argument form (`f {..}` as a call) reaches across the\n44: same spot whenever a definition's body *ends* in an identifier and the program body starts\n45: with `{` -- `... -> r * r` followed by `{a: ...}` reads the record as an argument to `r`. That\n46: form was justified as \"was a syntax error before, so nothing is taken away,\" which is true\n47: inside any delimiter and false at exactly this boundary. Until the boundary gets a real\n48: delimiter (or the grammar goes newline-sensitive), a program body cannot start with `[` after\n49: a definition, nor with `{` when the definition ends in an identifier -- worked around in the\n50: corpus by shaping bodies to avoid both.\n51: \n52: What is still open: this is a manual invariant with no structural enforcement, the same shape as\n53: [one invariant, three independent construction sites](one-invariant-three-independent-construction-sites.md)\n54: -- nothing stops a future grammar addition from introducing a new undelimited adjacency, or a new\n55: bracketed construct from forgetting to re-open the flag. The general lesson travels beyond this\n56: parser: whitespace-insensitive juxtaposition syntax is only as safe as the delimiter discipline of\n57: every boundary it can reach, and a grammar that has gotten away without delimiters somewhere (here,\n58: between definitions and the body) has been relying on no construct ever needing to look past that\n59: boundary -- which stops being true the moment one does.\n60: \n61: The fix itself was later ported onto `winnow` unchanged in shape, which is its own finding: see\n62: [winnow replaced the tokenizer, not the grammar](winnow-replaced-the-tokenizer-not-the-grammar.md).\n63: \n64: The flag did not survive bare application becoming the default call form (2026-08-28). It was\n65: replaced by the newline sensitivity the paragraph above named as the alternative: an argument --\n66: bare, parenthesized, braced, or a variant's payload -- must start on the same line as its\n67: function. One rule at the argument's start covers every call form at every boundary, where the\n68: flag covered one form and had to be re-threaded through five call sites; the record-argument\n69: and `(`-argument reaches, which the flag never covered, closed with it. The measurement that\n70: picked the line rule over suspending the sugar is on issue #10: suspension would have broken\n71: two live corpus cases, the line rule broke zero. What it does not cover is the postfix `[`\n72: instance above -- indexing is not an argument, and making postfix tokens line-sensitive would\n73: be a different (and larger) decision -- so a program body still cannot start with `[` directly\n74: after a definition, and the corpus workaround for that one stands.\n\n(End of file - total 74 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\ntype: Lesson\ncalendar:\n  - 2026-08-26\n  - 2026-08-28\ntitle: Juxtaposition is unsafe at any undelimited boundary\ndescription: Adding parenless function application broke a program with no application in it, because two unrelated expressions had always sat adjacent at one spot the grammar never bothered to delimit.\ntags:\n  - parsing\n  - syntax\n  - design\ntimestamp: 2026-08-26T00:00:00Z\n---\n\n`fn f(x: Int) -> Int = x` followed by `f(1)` stopped compiling the moment bare application\n(`f x` meaning `f(x)`) was added, with no bare application anywhere in the source. The error was\n`expected an expression, found end of program`: the whole program body had been consumed as the\nargument to `x`, the last token of `f`'s own definition.\n\nThe file grammar is `(fn ... | type ...)* body`. Every other place an expression is parsed fresh","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/research-log/juxtaposition-is-unsafe-at-any-undelimited-boundary.md","text":"---\ntype: Lesson\ncalendar:\n  - 2026-08-26\n  - 2026-08-28\ntitle: Juxtaposition is unsafe at any undelimited boundary\ndescription: Adding parenless function application broke a program with no application in it, because two unrelated expressions had always sat adjacent at one spot the grammar never bothered to delimit.\ntags:\n  - parsing\n  - syntax\n  - design\ntimestamp: 2026-08-26T00:00:00Z\n---\n\n`fn f(x: Int) -> Int = x` followed by `f(1)` stopped compiling the moment bare application\n(`f x` meaning `f(x)`) was added, with no bare application anywhere in the source. The error was\n`expected an expression, found end of program`: the whole program body had been consumed as the\nargument to `x`, the last token of `f`'s own definition.\n\nThe file grammar is `(fn ... | type ...)* body`. Every other place an expression is parsed fresh\n-- a pipe's right side, inside `(...)`/`[...]`/`{...}`, the program's own `body` -- is either\npreceded by a token that anchors it (`|`, `(`, `[`, `{`) or followed by one that bounds it (`)`,\n`]`, `}`, or `Eof` for `body`). A definition's own body is the one exception: nothing marks where\nit ends. The loop that reads definitions just keeps going while it sees `fn`/`type` and falls\nthrough to `body` when it does not, so a definition's last token sits directly next to whatever\ncomes next with no separator at all -- another definition (safe, because `fn`/`type` cannot start\na bare argument) or the program's own body (unsafe, because an identifier can).\n\nThe fix is a parser flag, off for exactly the undelimited top-level chain of a definition's body\nand restored the instant a real delimiter is entered (parens, brackets, braces), since a closing\ntoken bounds those regardless of what sits outside them. It has to be threaded through every\nplace `expr` is called fresh from inside such a delimiter -- five call sites in this parser --\nbecause forgetting one silently reopens the hazard for that one construct rather than failing\nloudly.\n\nA second instance surfaced while writing enum corpus cases, and the flag does not cover it:\n`fn g(s: Int) -> Int = 1` followed by a program body of `[g(1), g(2)]` fails with\n`expected ']', found ','`, because postfix indexing reaches across the same boundary and reads\nthe body's Vec literal as `1[g(1), ...]`. Bare application was suspended at that boundary;\n`[` was not, and suspending it the same way would break legitimate indexing inside a\ndefinition's body (`v[0]!`). Nothing in the corpus had ever put a `[`-literal body directly\nafter a definition, which is how it stayed invisible for as long as bare application's version\ndid. And a third: the parenless record-argument form (`f {..}` as a call) reaches across the\nsame spot whenever a definition's body *ends* in an identifier and the program body starts\nwith `{` -- `... -> r * r` followed by `{a: ...}` reads the record as an argument to `r`. That\nform was justified as \"was a syntax error before, so nothing is taken away,\" which is true\ninside any delimiter and false at exactly this boundary. Until the boundary gets a real\ndelimiter (or the grammar goes newline-sensitive), a program body cannot start with `[` after\na definition, nor with `{` when the definition ends in an identifier -- worked around in the\ncorpus by shaping bodies to avoid both.\n\nWhat is still open: this is a manual invariant with no structural enforcement, the same shape as\n[one invariant, three independent construction sites](one-invariant-three-independent-construction-sites.md)\n-- nothing stops a future grammar addition from introducing a new undelimited adjacency, or a new\nbracketed construct from forgetting to re-open the flag. The general lesson travels beyond this\nparser: whitespace-insensitive juxtaposition syntax is only as safe as the delimiter discipline of\nevery boundary it can reach, and a grammar that has gotten away without delimiters somewhere (here,\nbetween definitions and the body) has been relying on no construct ever needing to look past that\nboundary -- which stops being true the moment one does.\n\nThe fix itself was later ported onto `winnow` unchanged in shape, which is its own finding: see\n[winnow replaced the tokenizer, not the grammar](winnow-replaced-the-tokenizer-not-the-grammar.md).\n\nThe flag did not survive bare application becoming the default call form (2026-08-28). It was\nreplaced by the newline sensitivity the paragraph above named as the alternative: an argument --\nbare, parenthesized, braced, or a variant's payload -- must start on the same line as its\nfunction. One rule at the argument's start covers every call form at every boundary, where the\nflag covered one form and had to be re-threaded through five call sites; the record-argument\nand `(`-argument reaches, which the flag never covered, closed with it. The measurement that\npicked the line rule over suspending the sugar is on issue #10: suspension would have broken\ntwo live corpus cases, the line rule broke zero. What it does not cover is the postfix `[`\ninstance above -- indexing is not an argument, and making postfix tokens line-sensitive would\nbe a different (and larger) decision -- so a program body still cannot start with `[` directly\nafter a definition, and the corpus workaround for that one stands.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":74,"totalLines":74,"truncated":false}},"title":"research-log/juxtaposition-is-unsafe-at-any-undelimited-boundary.md","time":{"start":1788292195619,"end":1788292195622}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[]}},"id":"prt_05e856327001lAtuxdvtqvRmz1","sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","messageID":"msg_05e855741001YrThuT739047Ut"}}
