{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788292197091,"sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","part":{"id":"prt_05e856ae10010P7Zakgci5x57Y","messageID":"msg_05e856557001COWyocbS9cwtMz","sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","snapshot":"0ebf070b795b2e106627c5d8e519929a4e05dbe7","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788292216572,"sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_01a05e85b5bf701294a2a430","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"rg -l '^  input$' tests/corpus/*.yaml"},"output":"(no output)","metadata":{"output":"(no output)","exit":1,"truncated":false},"title":"rg -l '^  input$' tests/corpus/*.yaml","time":{"start":1788292216546,"end":1788292216569}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"This research log is very informative. The same-line rule was the fix for the juxtaposition hazard. The ruling now replaces it with the terminator.\n\nNow, the question of whether to retire the `[` same-line rule. The research log says \"making postfix tokens line-sensitive would be a different (and larger) decision\". The `[` rule was added later (issue #148) as a targeted fix for the vec-literal-after-definition case.\n\nWith the terminator, the `[` reach across the definition boundary is gone. So the `[` same-line rule can be retired. But the side effect: `[1, 2]\\n[0]` becomes `[1,2][0]` = 2 instead of a parse error.\n\nHmm, let me think about whether that's a problem. Actually, let me reconsider. The whole point of the ruling is \"the boundary becomes a token instead of a line-position fact.\" This suggests the language is moving away from line-sensitivity. Retiring the `[` rule is consistent with that.\n\nBut wait — is there a risk that retiring the `[` rule breaks a corpus case or docs fragment? Let me search for cases where a `[` follows an expression on a different line. Actually, the corpus cases are all formatted canonically, and the formatter always writes `[` right after its base (e.g., `v[0]`). So no corpus case would have `expr\\n[` patterns... except the vec-literal-body-after-definition case, which is now handled by the terminator.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether I should retire the `[` rule at all. The issue says \"retire the same-line rule and its diagnostic\". The `[` rule is a separate instance of line-sensitivity. Let me re-read the issue once more:\n\n\"Implementation: pick the terminator token (semicolon is the working example in the grill round; confirm during implementation it doesn't collide with anything else in the grammar), require it after every `fn` and `type` declaration, and retire the same-line same-line rule and its diagnostic once the terminator makes the ambiguity unreachable.\"\n\n\"retire the same-line rule and its diagnostic\" — the same-line rule for call arguments (issues #10, #20). The `[` rule (issue #148) is a different rule, though it uses the same `same_line` mechanism.\n\nHmm. Let me think about what's cleanest and most aligned with the ruling's intent. The ruling's intent: the declaration boundary is now a token. The same-line rule existed to protect the declaration boundary. Once the terminator protects it, the same-line rule is dead weight.\n\nFor the `[` rule: it also existed to protect the declaration boundary (issue #148). With the terminator, it's dead weight too. But retiring it has the `[1,2]\\n[0]` side effect.\n\nLet me consider: is `[1, 2]\\n[0]` a program anyone would write? It's two vec literals on two lines. As a program, that's meaningless (two expressions). Currently it's a parse error. If I retire the rule, it becomes `[1,2][0]` = 2. \n\nActually, you know what — let me check what the formatter does with `[1, 2]\\n[0]`. The formatter parses and re-renders. If the parser reads it as `[1,2][0]`, the formatter would render `[1, 2][0]`. So it'd be a valid program. Hmm.\n\nLet me look at whether there's a test that pins the `[` same-line behavior. The `vec_body_after_definition` corpus case pins that a vec literal body after a definition works. With the terminator, that case needs `;` added, and it still works. There's no test pinning that `[1,2]\\n[0]` is an error.\n\nOK let me make a decision. I'll retire the `[` same-line rule too, since:\n1. It's the same mechanism (`same_line`).\n2. It existed only to protect the declaration boundary, which the terminator now protects.\n3. The ruling's philosophy is \"boundary becomes a token instead of a line-position fact.\"\n\nActually wait. Let me reconsider once more. There's a subtlety. If I retire the `[` rule, then consider this program:\n```\nfn f(v: Vec<Int>) -> Vec<Int> = v\n\n[1, 2, 3]\n```\nWith terminator: `v;` ends f. Then `[1, 2, 3]` is the body. Good.\n\nBut what about:\n```\nfn f(v: Vec<Int>) -> Vec<Int> = v[0]\n\n[1, 2, 3]\n```\n`v[0]` is f's body, then `;`. Then `[1, 2, 3]` is the program body. Good.\n\nNow, the concern: what if someone writes a def body that ends in a vec literal, and the program body starts with `[`? Like:\n```\nfn f() -> Vec<Int> = [1, 2]\n\n[3, 4]\n```\nWith terminator: `[1, 2];` ends f. Then `[3, 4]` is the body. Good. Without the terminator and without the `[` rule, `[1, 2]` would swallow `[3, 4]` as an index → `[1,2][3,4]` → error. But with the terminator, it's fine.\n\nOK so retiring the `[` rule is safe with the terminator. Let me do it.\n\nHmm wait, but actually — let me reconsider whether retiring the `[` rule could break the `input` handling or anything else. The `[` rule is in `postfix`. If I remove it, `postfix` always takes `[` as an index/project/slice. That's the pre-#148 behavior. Fine.\n\nNow, let me also reconsider the `input <type>` disambiguation once more. Actually, I realize there might be a subtle issue. Let me look at the `input` handling again:\n\n```rust\nlet input = match p.peek()? {\n    (Tok::Input, input_span) => {\n        let (next, next_span) = p.peek2()?;\n        if (next == Tok::LBrace || matches!(next, Tok::Ident(_)))\n            && p.same_line(input_span.end, next_span.start)\n        {\n            p.advance()?;\n            Some(p.type_expr()?)\n        } else {\n            None\n        }\n    }\n    _ => None,\n};\n```\n\nIf I remove the same_line check, then `input` followed by `{` or `Ident` is always a declaration. Let me think about whether this could break a valid program.\n\nThe only valid programs where `input` (as a value) is the FIRST token of the program body:\n1. `input` alone as the body.\n2. `input` as part of a larger expression, e.g. `f(input)`, `input | map(...)`, `{a: input}`, `[input]`, `input + 1`, etc.\n\nFor case 1: `input` alone. Next token after `input` is Eof. Not `{` or `Ident`. So it's the body. Good.\n\nFor case 2: `input` is followed by `)`, `|`, `}`, `]`, `+`, etc. — none of which are `{` or `Ident`. So it's the body. Good.\n\nWait, but what about `input` followed by a bare call? Like `input f`? No, `input` is not a function. `input f` would be... hmm, `input` as a value followed by `f` — that's not valid. And with the new rule, `input f` at the top would be parsed as `input <type f>` declaration. Then body is empty → error. Either way it's an error.\n\nWhat about `input` followed by a record literal as a call argument? Like `input {a: 1}`? `input` is not a function, so `input {a: 1}` is not a call. Currently: `input` value, then `{a: 1}` → parse error. With new rule: `input {a: 1}` → declaration `input` of type `{a: 1}` → `1` is not a type → parse error. Either way error.\n\nHmm, wait, but there's one more case. What if the program body is a pipeline that starts with `input` and the pipe is on the next line?\n```\ninput\n| map(. + 1)\n```\n`input` followed by `|`. Not `{` or `Ident`. So it's the body. Good.\n\nOK so removing the same_line check from the `input` handling is safe for valid programs. But actually — hold on. Let me reconsider. What if the program body is `input` followed by a call on the next line where `input` is the argument? Like:\n```\nf(\n    input\n)\n```\nNo, that's inside parens, not at the top.\n\nWhat about:\n```\ninput\nf(input)\n```\n`input` followed by `f` (an Ident) on the next line. With the new rule, this is parsed as `input <type f>` declaration, then body = `(input)`. Wait, `f` is read as a named type. Then the declaration is done. Then `let body = p.expr(0)?` reads `(input)`. Hmm, that's `input` in parens. So the program would be: `input` declared as type `f`, body `(input)`. The checker would fail because `f` is a function, not a type. So it's an error, but a different error than before.\n\nBefore: `input` value, then `f(input)` — two expressions → \"expected end of program, found `f`\". After: \"expected a type, found...\" no wait, `f` IS a valid named type syntactically. So the checker says something like \"`f` is not a type\" or \"cannot tell what `input` contains\". Either way, an error. No valid program breaks.\n\nOK, I'm now fairly confident. Let me also double check: is there any corpus case or docs fragment where the program body starts with `input` followed by `{` or `Ident` on the next line? Let me search.\n\nActually, let me search the corpus for programs that start with `input` as the body.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05e85b631001aLSWAUU0XM3kjG","sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","messageID":"msg_05e856557001COWyocbS9cwtMz"}}
