{"type":"text","timestamp":1788292244450,"sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","part":{"id":"prt_05e86231b001kd8jB1nML9N2Jl","messageID":"msg_05e85dcfc001LtZWMfGMidPwU3","sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","type":"text","text":"\n\n","time":{"start":1788292244251,"end":1788292244449}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788292244458,"sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_c5a47b595013408b8601dc30","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/build.rs"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/build.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: //! Regenerates prelude.toy's checked form on every build (kantord/toylang#73), so `prelude::checked`\n2: //! can hand back its functions as already-typed `tir::Func` values instead of the library parsing\n3: //! and type-checking prelude.toy itself on every compile. Reuses the library's own parser and\n4: //! checker rather than duplicating their logic: `ast`, `error`, `ty`, `tir`, `parse`, and `check`\n5: //! below are the same source files `src/lib.rs` compiles, pulled in as this script's own modules\n6: //! because a build script cannot depend on the crate it is building. None of them touch a backend,\n7: //! `main.rs`, or anything else outside this closed set, so there is nothing else to pull in.\n8: //!\n9: //! What comes out is not those checked values themselves (this process ends when the script\n10: //! does) but Rust source text that reconstructs them: a `ToRust` impl per type below renders each\n11: //! one as the literal expression that builds it, so `prelude::checked` gets back a plain\n12: //! `Vec<tir::Func>`, no parsing, deserialization, or unsafe transmute involved.\n13: //!\n14: //! A `Kind` field that is `Box<Tir>` in `tir.rs` still passes as plain `&Tir` to the helpers\n15: //! below (deref coercion) -- `boxed()` re-adds the `Box::new(...)` explicitly, at the one call\n16: //! site that field needs it, rather than a helper declaring `&Box<Tir>` to get it automatically.\n17: \n18: #[path = \"src/ast.rs\"]\n19: mod ast;\n20: #[path = \"src/check/mod.rs\"]\n21: mod check;\n22: #[path = \"src/error.rs\"]\n23: mod error;\n24: #[path = \"src/parse.rs\"]\n25: mod parse;\n26: #[path = \"src/tir.rs\"]\n27: mod tir;\n28: #[path = \"src/ty.rs\"]\n29: mod ty;\n30: \n31: /// `check::mod`'s `check` function (unused here -- this script only calls `check::check_module`)\n32: /// calls `crate::prelude::checked()`, so that name has to resolve for the file to compile at\n33: /// all. The real `src/prelude.rs` can't fill in for it: its own `checked()` is generated from\n34: /// the very file this script is producing. An empty stand-in is enough, since `check` never\n35: /// actually runs in this binary.\n36: mod prelude {\n37:     pub fn checked() -> Vec<crate::tir::Func> {\n38:         Vec::new()\n39:     }\n40: }\n41: \n42: trait ToRust {\n43:     /// The Rust expression that builds a value equal to this one.\n44:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String;\n45: }\n46: \n47: impl ToRust for String {\n48:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n49:         format!(\"{self:?}.to_string()\")\n50:     }\n51: }\n52: \n53: impl ToRust for str {\n54:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n55:         format!(\"{self:?}\")\n56:     }\n57: }\n58: \n59: impl ToRust for bool {\n60:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n61:         self.to_string()\n62:     }\n63: }\n64: \n65: impl ToRust for i64 {\n66:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n67:         format!(\"{self}i64\")\n68:     }\n69: }\n70: \n71: impl ToRust for u32 {\n72:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n73:         format!(\"{self}u32\")\n74:     }\n75: }\n76: \n77: impl ToRust for usize {\n78:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n79:         format!(\"{self}usize\")\n80:     }\n81: }\n82: \n83: impl<T: ToRust> ToRust for Option<T> {\n84:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n85:         match self {\n86:             Some(v) => format!(\"Some({})\", v.to_rust()),\n87:             None => \"None\".to_string(),\n88:         }\n89:     }\n90: }\n91: \n92: impl<T: ToRust> ToRust for Box<T> {\n93:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n94:         format!(\"Box::new({})\", (**self).to_rust())\n95:     }\n96: }\n97: \n98: impl<T: ToRust> ToRust for [T] {\n99:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n100:         let items: Vec<String> = self.iter().map(ToRust::to_rust).collect();\n101:         format!(\"vec![{}]\", items.join(\", \"))\n102:     }\n103: }\n104: \n105: impl<A: ToRust, B: ToRust> ToRust for (A, B) {\n106:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n107:         format!(\"({}, {})\", self.0.to_rust(), self.1.to_rust())\n108:     }\n109: }\n110: \n111: impl ToRust for ast::BinOp {\n112:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n113:         let variant = match self {\n114:             ast::BinOp::Add => \"Add\",\n115:             ast::BinOp::Sub => \"Sub\",\n116:             ast::BinOp::Mul => \"Mul\",\n117:             ast::BinOp::Div => \"Div\",\n118:             ast::BinOp::Rem => \"Rem\",\n119:             ast::BinOp::Eq => \"Eq\",\n120:             ast::BinOp::Ne => \"Ne\",\n121:             ast::BinOp::Lt => \"Lt\",\n122:             ast::BinOp::Le => \"Le\",\n123:             ast::BinOp::Gt => \"Gt\",\n124:             ast::BinOp::Ge => \"Ge\",\n125:         };\n126:         format!(\"crate::ast::BinOp::{variant}\")\n127:     }\n128: }\n129: \n130: impl ToRust for ast::LogicOp {\n131:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n132:         let variant = match self {\n133:             ast::LogicOp::And => \"And\",\n134:             ast::LogicOp::Or => \"Or\",\n135:         };\n136:         format!(\"crate::ast::LogicOp::{variant}\")\n137:     }\n138: }\n139: \n140: impl ToRust for ty::Type {\n141:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n142:         match self {\n143:             ty::Type::Str => \"crate::ty::Type::Str\".to_string(),\n144:             ty::Type::Int => \"crate::ty::Type::Int\".to_string(),\n145:             ty::Type::Int64 => \"crate::ty::Type::Int64\".to_string(),\n146:             ty::Type::Bool => \"crate::ty::Type::Bool\".to_string(),\n147:             ty::Type::Char => \"crate::ty::Type::Char\".to_string(),\n148:             ty::Type::Vec(elem) => format!(\"crate::ty::Type::Vec({})\", elem.to_rust()),\n149:             ty::Type::Stream(elem) => format!(\"crate::ty::Type::Stream({})\", elem.to_rust()),\n150:             ty::Type::Sink => \"crate::ty::Type::Sink\".to_string(),\n151:             ty::Type::Record(fields) => {\n152:                 format!(\"crate::ty::Type::Record({})\", fields.to_rust())\n153:             }\n154:             ty::Type::Enum {\n155:                 name,\n156:                 args,\n157:                 variants,\n158:             } => format!(\n159:                 \"crate::ty::Type::Enum {{ name: {}, args: {}, variants: {} }}\",\n160:                 name.to_rust(),\n161:                 args.to_rust(),\n162:                 variants.to_rust(),\n163:             ),\n164:             ty::Type::Param(name) => format!(\"crate::ty::Type::Param({})\", name.to_rust()),\n165:         }\n166:     }\n167: }\n168: \n169: impl ToRust for tir::Builtin {\n170:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n171:         let variant = match self {\n172:             tir::Builtin::IntToStr => \"IntToStr\",\n173:             tir::Builtin::IntToI64 => \"IntToI64\",\n174:             tir::Builtin::Range => \"Range\",\n175:             tir::Builtin::Collect => \"Collect\",\n176:             tir::Builtin::JsonLines => \"JsonLines\",\n177:             tir::Builtin::Length => \"Length\",\n178:             tir::Builtin::Flatten => \"Flatten\",\n179:             tir::Builtin::Tail => \"Tail\",\n180:             tir::Builtin::Fields => \"Fields\",\n181:             tir::Builtin::Chars => \"Chars\",\n182:             tir::Builtin::Sort => \"Sort\",\n183:             tir::Builtin::Reverse => \"Reverse\",\n184:             tir::Builtin::Sum => \"Sum\",\n185:             tir::Builtin::Max => \"Max\",\n186:         };\n187:         format!(\"crate::tir::Builtin::{variant}\")\n188:     }\n189: }\n190: \n191: impl ToRust for tir::Tir {\n192:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n193:         format!(\n194:             \"crate::tir::Tir::new({}, {})\",\n195:             self.ty.to_rust(),\n196:             self.kind.to_rust()\n197:         )\n198:     }\n199: }\n200: \n201: impl ToRust for tir::MatchArm {\n202:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n203:         format!(\n204:             \"crate::tir::MatchArm {{ variant: {}, guard: {}, payload: {}, body: {} }}\",\n205:             self.variant.to_rust(),\n206:             self.guard.to_rust(),\n207:             self.payload.to_rust(),\n208:             self.body.to_rust(),\n209:         )\n210:     }\n211: }\n212: \n213: /// `crate::tir::Kind::{name} { k1: v1, k2: v2, ... }`, the shape shared by every struct-like\n214: /// `Kind` variant. One small function per variant below builds its own field list and calls\n215: /// this, which is what keeps the dispatching match in `Kind::to_rust` to one line per variant --\n216: /// laid out as a table, not a 100-line function apiece.\n217: fn variant(name: &str, fields: &[(&str, String)]) -> String {\n218:     let rendered: Vec<String> = fields.iter().map(|(k, v)| format!(\"{k}: {v}\")).collect();\n219:     format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::{name} {{ {} }}\", rendered.join(\", \"))\n220: }\n221: \n222: /// `tir.rs` stores a `Tir` child as `Box<Tir>`; this is the `Box::new(...)` that reconstructs\n223: /// it, called explicitly at each field that needs it rather than a helper below taking `&Box<Tir>`\n224: /// to get the wrapping automatically -- a plain `&Tir` parameter is what a `Box<Tir>` field\n225: /// coerces to at the call site regardless, so declaring the box type would buy nothing.\n226: fn boxed(t: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n227:     format!(\"Box::new({})\", t.to_rust())\n228: }\n229: \n230: fn record_lit(fields: &[(String, tir::Tir)]) -> String {\n231:     variant(\"RecordLit\", &[(\"fields\", fields.to_rust())])\n232: }\n233: fn enum_lit(variant_name: &String, payload: &Option<Box<tir::Tir>>) -> String {\n234:     variant(\n235:         \"EnumLit\",\n236:         &[\n237:             (\"variant\", variant_name.to_rust()),\n238:             (\"payload\", payload.to_rust()),\n239:         ],\n240:     )\n241: }\n242: fn opt_map(source: &tir::Tir, param: &tir::LocalId, body: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n243:     variant(\n244:         \"OptMap\",\n245:         &[\n246:             (\"source\", boxed(source)),\n247:             (\"param\", param.to_rust()),\n248:             (\"body\", boxed(body)),\n249:         ],\n250:     )\n251: }\n252: fn call(func: &String, arg: &Option<Box<tir::Tir>>) -> String {\n253:     variant(\"Call\", &[(\"func\", func.to_rust()), (\"arg\", arg.to_rust())])\n254: }\n255: fn arith(op: &ast::BinOp, lhs: &tir::Tir, rhs: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n256:     variant(\n257:         \"Arith\",\n258:         &[\n259:             (\"op\", op.to_rust()),\n260:             (\"lhs\", boxed(lhs)),\n261:             (\"rhs\", boxed(rhs)),\n262:         ],\n263:     )\n264: }\n265: fn cond(cond: &tir::Tir, then: &tir::Tir, otherwise: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n266:     variant(\n267:         \"Cond\",\n268:         &[\n269:             (\"cond\", boxed(cond)),\n270:             (\"then\", boxed(then)),\n271:             (\"otherwise\", boxed(otherwise)),\n272:         ],\n273:     )\n274: }\n275: fn compare(op: &ast::BinOp, lhs: &tir::Tir, rhs: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n276:     variant(\n277:         \"Compare\",\n278:         &[\n279:             (\"op\", op.to_rust()),\n280:             (\"lhs\", boxed(lhs)),\n281:             (\"rhs\", boxed(rhs)),\n282:         ],\n283:     )\n284: }\n285: fn logic(op: &ast::LogicOp, lhs: &tir::Tir, rhs: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n286:     variant(\n287:         \"Logic\",\n288:         &[\n289:             (\"op\", op.to_rust()),\n290:             (\"lhs\", boxed(lhs)),\n291:             (\"rhs\", boxed(rhs)),\n292:         ],\n293:     )\n294: }\n295: fn bind(local: &tir::LocalId, value: &tir::Tir, body: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n296:     variant(\n297:         \"Bind\",\n298:         &[\n299:             (\"local\", local.to_rust()),\n300:             (\"value\", boxed(value)),\n301:             (\"body\", boxed(body)),\n302:         ],\n303:     )\n304: }\n305: fn map(source: &tir::Tir, param: &tir::LocalId, body: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n306:     variant(\n307:         \"Map\",\n308:         &[\n309:             (\"source\", boxed(source)),\n310:             (\"param\", param.to_rust()),\n311:             (\"body\", boxed(body)),\n312:         ],\n313:     )\n314: }\n315: fn select(source: &tir::Tir, param: &tir::LocalId, pred: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n316:     variant(\n317:         \"Select\",\n318:         &[\n319:             (\"source\", boxed(source)),\n320:             (\"param\", param.to_rust()),\n321:             (\"pred\", boxed(pred)),\n322:         ],\n323:     )\n324: }\n325: fn field(base: &tir::Tir, name: &String) -> String {\n326:     variant(\"Field\", &[(\"base\", boxed(base)), (\"name\", name.to_rust())])\n327: }\n328: fn builtin(which: &tir::Builtin, arg: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n329:     variant(\n330:         \"Builtin\",\n331:         &[(\"which\", which.to_rust()), (\"arg\", boxed(arg))],\n332:     )\n333: }\n334: fn index(base: &tir::Tir, index: &tir::Tir, depth: &usize, elem_is_record: &bool) -> String {\n335:     variant(\n336:         \"Index\",\n337:         &[\n338:             (\"base\", boxed(base)),\n339:             (\"index\", boxed(index)),\n340:             (\"depth\", depth.to_rust()),\n341:             (\"elem_is_record\", elem_is_record.to_rust()),\n342:         ],\n343:     )\n344: }\n345: fn slice(\n346:     base: &tir::Tir,\n347:     start: &Option<Box<tir::Tir>>,\n348:     end: &Option<Box<tir::Tir>>,\n349:     depth: &usize,\n350: ) -> String {\n351:     variant(\n352:         \"Slice\",\n353:         &[\n354:             (\"base\", boxed(base)),\n355:             (\"start\", start.to_rust()),\n356:             (\"end\", end.to_rust()),\n357:             (\"depth\", depth.to_rust()),\n358:         ],\n359:     )\n360: }\n361: fn match_(subject: &tir::Tir, arms: &[tir::MatchArm], partial: &bool) -> String {\n362:     variant(\n363:         \"Match\",\n364:         &[\n365:             (\"subject\", boxed(subject)),\n366:             (\"arms\", arms.to_rust()),\n367:             (\"partial\", partial.to_rust()),\n368:         ],\n369:     )\n370: }\n371: \n372: /// Exhaustive on purpose: a `Kind` variant this does not know renders nothing rather than\n373: /// something wrong, and a match with no wildcard arm is what turns that into a build error the\n374: /// moment the checker can produce it, naming exactly the arm to add (kantord/toylang#73's\n375: /// \"independent of its growth\").\n376: impl ToRust for tir::Kind {\n377:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n378:         use tir::Kind::*;\n379:         match self {\n380:             Str(s) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Str({})\", s.to_rust()),\n381:             Int(n) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Int({})\", n.to_rust()),\n382:             VecLit(items) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::VecLit({})\", items.to_rust()),\n383:             RecordLit { fields } => record_lit(fields),\n384:             EnumLit { variant, payload } => enum_lit(variant, payload),\n385:             OptMap {\n386:                 source,\n387:                 param,\n388:                 body,\n389:             } => opt_map(source, param, body),\n390:             Var(name) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Var({})\", name.to_rust()),\n391:             Local(id) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Local({})\", id.to_rust()),\n392:             Input => \"crate::tir::Kind::Input\".to_string(),\n393:             Inputs => \"crate::tir::Kind::Inputs\".to_string(),\n394:             Lines => \"crate::tir::Kind::Lines\".to_string(),\n395:             Dsv { delim } => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Dsv {{ delim: {} }}\", delim.to_rust()),\n396:             Call { func, arg } => call(func, arg),\n397:             Concat(a, b) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Concat({}, {})\", a.to_rust(), b.to_rust()),\n398:             Arith { op, lhs, rhs } => arith(op, lhs, rhs),\n399:             Cond {\n400:                 cond: c,\n401:                 then,\n402:                 otherwise,\n403:             } => cond(c, then, otherwise),\n404:             Compare { op, lhs, rhs } => compare(op, lhs, rhs),\n405:             Logic { op, lhs, rhs } => logic(op, lhs, rhs),\n406:             Not(base) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Not({})\", boxed(base)),\n407:             Bind { local, value, body } => bind(local, value, body),\n408:             Map {\n409:                 source,\n410:                 param,\n411:                 body,\n412:             } => map(source, param, body),\n413:             Select {\n414:                 source,\n415:                 param,\n416:                 pred,\n417:             } => select(source, param, pred),\n418:             Field { base, name } => field(base, name),\n419:             Builtin { which, arg } => builtin(which, arg),\n420:             Unwrap { base } => variant(\"Unwrap\", &[(\"base\", base.to_rust())]),\n421:             Index {\n422:                 base,\n423:                 index: i,\n424:                 depth,\n425:                 elem_is_record,\n426:             } => index(base, i, depth, elem_is_record),\n427:             Slice {\n428:                 base,\n429:                 start,\n430:                 end,\n431:                 depth,\n432:             } => slice(base, start, end, depth),\n433:             Match {\n434:                 subject,\n435:                 arms,\n436:                 partial,\n437:             } => match_(subject, arms, partial),\n438:         }\n439:     }\n440: }\n441: \n442: impl ToRust for tir::Func {\n443:     fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n444:         format!(\n445:             \"crate::tir::Func {{ name: {}, param: {}, param_ty: {}, body: {} }}\",\n446:             self.name.to_rust(),\n447:             self.param.to_rust(),\n448:             self.param_ty.to_rust(),\n449:             self.body.to_rust(),\n450:         )\n451:     }\n452: }\n453: \n454: fn main() {\n455:     println!(\"cargo::rerun-if-changed=prelude.toy\");\n456:     for src in [\n457:         \"src/ast.rs\",\n458:         \"src/error.rs\",\n459:         \"src/parse.rs\",\n460:         \"src/tir.rs\",\n461:         \"src/ty.rs\",\n462:         \"src/check/mod.rs\",\n463:         \"src/check/types.rs\",\n464:         \"src/check/linearity.rs\",\n465:     ] {\n466:         println!(\"cargo::rerun-if-changed={src}\");\n467:     }\n468: \n469:     let src = std::fs::read_to_string(\"prelude.toy\").expect(\"prelude.toy is readable\");\n470:     let mut module = parse::parse_module(&src).expect(\"prelude.toy is valid toylang\");\n471:     // Mark prelude origin the way `prelude::module()` does, so a `pub` function may call a\n472:     // private helper without tripping the checker's cross-file visibility rule. Private\n473:     // definitions are checked here too, not just `pub` ones, so a broken helper is caught at\n474:     // build time rather than whenever a `pub` function first reaches it.\n475:     for def in &mut module.defs {\n476:         def.origin = ast::Origin::Prelude;\n477:     }\n478:     let module = ast::Module {\n479:         defs: module.defs,\n480:         enums: module.enums.into_iter().filter(|e| e.is_pub).collect(),\n481:     };\n482:     let funcs = check::check_module(&module).expect(\"prelude.toy checks\");\n483: \n484:     let generated = format!(\n485:         \"/// Every `fn` in prelude.toy, already checked, `pub` and private alike. Generated by \\\n486:          build.rs from prelude.toy -- edit that, not this.\\n\\\n487:          pub fn checked() -> Vec<crate::tir::Func> {{\\n    {}\\n}}\\n\",\n488:         funcs.to_rust(),\n489:     );\n490:     let out_dir = std::env::var(\"OUT_DIR\").expect(\"cargo sets OUT_DIR for a build script\");\n491:     std::fs::write(\n492:         std::path::Path::new(&out_dir).join(\"prelude_checked.rs\"),\n493:         generated,\n494:     )\n495:     .expect(\"can write the generated prelude source\");\n496: }\n\n(End of file - total 496 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"//! Regenerates prelude.toy's checked form on every build (kantord/toylang#73), so `prelude::checked`\n//! can hand back its functions as already-typed `tir::Func` values instead of the library parsing\n//! and type-checking prelude.toy itself on every compile. Reuses the library's own parser and\n//! checker rather than duplicating their logic: `ast`, `error`, `ty`, `tir`, `parse`, and `check`\n//! below are the same source files `src/lib.rs` compiles, pulled in as this script's own modules\n//! because a build script cannot depend on the crate it is building. None of them touch a backend,\n//! `main.rs`, or anything else outside this closed set, so there is nothing else to pull in.\n//!\n//! What comes out is not those checked values themselves (this process ends when the script\n//! does) but Rust source text that reconstructs them: a `ToRust` impl per type below renders each\n//! one as the literal expression that builds it, so `prelude::checked` gets back a plain\n//! `Vec<tir::Func>`, no parsing, deserialization, or unsafe transmute involved.\n//!\n//! A `Kind` field that is `Box<Tir>` in `tir.rs` still passes as plain `&Tir` to the helpers\n//! below (deref coercion) -- `boxed()` re-adds the `Box::new(...)` explicitly, at the one call\n//! site that field needs it, rather than a helper declaring `&Box<Tir>` to get it automatically.\n\n#[path = \"src/ast.rs\"]\nmod ast;\n#[path = \"src/check/mod.rs\"]","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/build.rs","text":"//! Regenerates prelude.toy's checked form on every build (kantord/toylang#73), so `prelude::checked`\n//! can hand back its functions as already-typed `tir::Func` values instead of the library parsing\n//! and type-checking prelude.toy itself on every compile. Reuses the library's own parser and\n//! checker rather than duplicating their logic: `ast`, `error`, `ty`, `tir`, `parse`, and `check`\n//! below are the same source files `src/lib.rs` compiles, pulled in as this script's own modules\n//! because a build script cannot depend on the crate it is building. None of them touch a backend,\n//! `main.rs`, or anything else outside this closed set, so there is nothing else to pull in.\n//!\n//! What comes out is not those checked values themselves (this process ends when the script\n//! does) but Rust source text that reconstructs them: a `ToRust` impl per type below renders each\n//! one as the literal expression that builds it, so `prelude::checked` gets back a plain\n//! `Vec<tir::Func>`, no parsing, deserialization, or unsafe transmute involved.\n//!\n//! A `Kind` field that is `Box<Tir>` in `tir.rs` still passes as plain `&Tir` to the helpers\n//! below (deref coercion) -- `boxed()` re-adds the `Box::new(...)` explicitly, at the one call\n//! site that field needs it, rather than a helper declaring `&Box<Tir>` to get it automatically.\n\n#[path = \"src/ast.rs\"]\nmod ast;\n#[path = \"src/check/mod.rs\"]\nmod check;\n#[path = \"src/error.rs\"]\nmod error;\n#[path = \"src/parse.rs\"]\nmod parse;\n#[path = \"src/tir.rs\"]\nmod tir;\n#[path = \"src/ty.rs\"]\nmod ty;\n\n/// `check::mod`'s `check` function (unused here -- this script only calls `check::check_module`)\n/// calls `crate::prelude::checked()`, so that name has to resolve for the file to compile at\n/// all. The real `src/prelude.rs` can't fill in for it: its own `checked()` is generated from\n/// the very file this script is producing. An empty stand-in is enough, since `check` never\n/// actually runs in this binary.\nmod prelude {\n    pub fn checked() -> Vec<crate::tir::Func> {\n        Vec::new()\n    }\n}\n\ntrait ToRust {\n    /// The Rust expression that builds a value equal to this one.\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String;\n}\n\nimpl ToRust for String {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\"{self:?}.to_string()\")\n    }\n}\n\nimpl ToRust for str {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\"{self:?}\")\n    }\n}\n\nimpl ToRust for bool {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        self.to_string()\n    }\n}\n\nimpl ToRust for i64 {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\"{self}i64\")\n    }\n}\n\nimpl ToRust for u32 {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\"{self}u32\")\n    }\n}\n\nimpl ToRust for usize {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\"{self}usize\")\n    }\n}\n\nimpl<T: ToRust> ToRust for Option<T> {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        match self {\n            Some(v) => format!(\"Some({})\", v.to_rust()),\n            None => \"None\".to_string(),\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl<T: ToRust> ToRust for Box<T> {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\"Box::new({})\", (**self).to_rust())\n    }\n}\n\nimpl<T: ToRust> ToRust for [T] {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        let items: Vec<String> = self.iter().map(ToRust::to_rust).collect();\n        format!(\"vec![{}]\", items.join(\", \"))\n    }\n}\n\nimpl<A: ToRust, B: ToRust> ToRust for (A, B) {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\"({}, {})\", self.0.to_rust(), self.1.to_rust())\n    }\n}\n\nimpl ToRust for ast::BinOp {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        let variant = match self {\n            ast::BinOp::Add => \"Add\",\n            ast::BinOp::Sub => \"Sub\",\n            ast::BinOp::Mul => \"Mul\",\n            ast::BinOp::Div => \"Div\",\n            ast::BinOp::Rem => \"Rem\",\n            ast::BinOp::Eq => \"Eq\",\n            ast::BinOp::Ne => \"Ne\",\n            ast::BinOp::Lt => \"Lt\",\n            ast::BinOp::Le => \"Le\",\n            ast::BinOp::Gt => \"Gt\",\n            ast::BinOp::Ge => \"Ge\",\n        };\n        format!(\"crate::ast::BinOp::{variant}\")\n    }\n}\n\nimpl ToRust for ast::LogicOp {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        let variant = match self {\n            ast::LogicOp::And => \"And\",\n            ast::LogicOp::Or => \"Or\",\n        };\n        format!(\"crate::ast::LogicOp::{variant}\")\n    }\n}\n\nimpl ToRust for ty::Type {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        match self {\n            ty::Type::Str => \"crate::ty::Type::Str\".to_string(),\n            ty::Type::Int => \"crate::ty::Type::Int\".to_string(),\n            ty::Type::Int64 => \"crate::ty::Type::Int64\".to_string(),\n            ty::Type::Bool => \"crate::ty::Type::Bool\".to_string(),\n            ty::Type::Char => \"crate::ty::Type::Char\".to_string(),\n            ty::Type::Vec(elem) => format!(\"crate::ty::Type::Vec({})\", elem.to_rust()),\n            ty::Type::Stream(elem) => format!(\"crate::ty::Type::Stream({})\", elem.to_rust()),\n            ty::Type::Sink => \"crate::ty::Type::Sink\".to_string(),\n            ty::Type::Record(fields) => {\n                format!(\"crate::ty::Type::Record({})\", fields.to_rust())\n            }\n            ty::Type::Enum {\n                name,\n                args,\n                variants,\n            } => format!(\n                \"crate::ty::Type::Enum {{ name: {}, args: {}, variants: {} }}\",\n                name.to_rust(),\n                args.to_rust(),\n                variants.to_rust(),\n            ),\n            ty::Type::Param(name) => format!(\"crate::ty::Type::Param({})\", name.to_rust()),\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl ToRust for tir::Builtin {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        let variant = match self {\n            tir::Builtin::IntToStr => \"IntToStr\",\n            tir::Builtin::IntToI64 => \"IntToI64\",\n            tir::Builtin::Range => \"Range\",\n            tir::Builtin::Collect => \"Collect\",\n            tir::Builtin::JsonLines => \"JsonLines\",\n            tir::Builtin::Length => \"Length\",\n            tir::Builtin::Flatten => \"Flatten\",\n            tir::Builtin::Tail => \"Tail\",\n            tir::Builtin::Fields => \"Fields\",\n            tir::Builtin::Chars => \"Chars\",\n            tir::Builtin::Sort => \"Sort\",\n            tir::Builtin::Reverse => \"Reverse\",\n            tir::Builtin::Sum => \"Sum\",\n            tir::Builtin::Max => \"Max\",\n        };\n        format!(\"crate::tir::Builtin::{variant}\")\n    }\n}\n\nimpl ToRust for tir::Tir {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\n            \"crate::tir::Tir::new({}, {})\",\n            self.ty.to_rust(),\n            self.kind.to_rust()\n        )\n    }\n}\n\nimpl ToRust for tir::MatchArm {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\n            \"crate::tir::MatchArm {{ variant: {}, guard: {}, payload: {}, body: {} }}\",\n            self.variant.to_rust(),\n            self.guard.to_rust(),\n            self.payload.to_rust(),\n            self.body.to_rust(),\n        )\n    }\n}\n\n/// `crate::tir::Kind::{name} { k1: v1, k2: v2, ... }`, the shape shared by every struct-like\n/// `Kind` variant. One small function per variant below builds its own field list and calls\n/// this, which is what keeps the dispatching match in `Kind::to_rust` to one line per variant --\n/// laid out as a table, not a 100-line function apiece.\nfn variant(name: &str, fields: &[(&str, String)]) -> String {\n    let rendered: Vec<String> = fields.iter().map(|(k, v)| format!(\"{k}: {v}\")).collect();\n    format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::{name} {{ {} }}\", rendered.join(\", \"))\n}\n\n/// `tir.rs` stores a `Tir` child as `Box<Tir>`; this is the `Box::new(...)` that reconstructs\n/// it, called explicitly at each field that needs it rather than a helper below taking `&Box<Tir>`\n/// to get the wrapping automatically -- a plain `&Tir` parameter is what a `Box<Tir>` field\n/// coerces to at the call site regardless, so declaring the box type would buy nothing.\nfn boxed(t: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n    format!(\"Box::new({})\", t.to_rust())\n}\n\nfn record_lit(fields: &[(String, tir::Tir)]) -> String {\n    variant(\"RecordLit\", &[(\"fields\", fields.to_rust())])\n}\nfn enum_lit(variant_name: &String, payload: &Option<Box<tir::Tir>>) -> String {\n    variant(\n        \"EnumLit\",\n        &[\n            (\"variant\", variant_name.to_rust()),\n            (\"payload\", payload.to_rust()),\n        ],\n    )\n}\nfn opt_map(source: &tir::Tir, param: &tir::LocalId, body: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n    variant(\n        \"OptMap\",\n        &[\n            (\"source\", boxed(source)),\n            (\"param\", param.to_rust()),\n            (\"body\", boxed(body)),\n        ],\n    )\n}\nfn call(func: &String, arg: &Option<Box<tir::Tir>>) -> String {\n    variant(\"Call\", &[(\"func\", func.to_rust()), (\"arg\", arg.to_rust())])\n}\nfn arith(op: &ast::BinOp, lhs: &tir::Tir, rhs: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n    variant(\n        \"Arith\",\n        &[\n            (\"op\", op.to_rust()),\n            (\"lhs\", boxed(lhs)),\n            (\"rhs\", boxed(rhs)),\n        ],\n    )\n}\nfn cond(cond: &tir::Tir, then: &tir::Tir, otherwise: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n    variant(\n        \"Cond\",\n        &[\n            (\"cond\", boxed(cond)),\n            (\"then\", boxed(then)),\n            (\"otherwise\", boxed(otherwise)),\n        ],\n    )\n}\nfn compare(op: &ast::BinOp, lhs: &tir::Tir, rhs: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n    variant(\n        \"Compare\",\n        &[\n            (\"op\", op.to_rust()),\n            (\"lhs\", boxed(lhs)),\n            (\"rhs\", boxed(rhs)),\n        ],\n    )\n}\nfn logic(op: &ast::LogicOp, lhs: &tir::Tir, rhs: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n    variant(\n        \"Logic\",\n        &[\n            (\"op\", op.to_rust()),\n            (\"lhs\", boxed(lhs)),\n            (\"rhs\", boxed(rhs)),\n        ],\n    )\n}\nfn bind(local: &tir::LocalId, value: &tir::Tir, body: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n    variant(\n        \"Bind\",\n        &[\n            (\"local\", local.to_rust()),\n            (\"value\", boxed(value)),\n            (\"body\", boxed(body)),\n        ],\n    )\n}\nfn map(source: &tir::Tir, param: &tir::LocalId, body: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n    variant(\n        \"Map\",\n        &[\n            (\"source\", boxed(source)),\n            (\"param\", param.to_rust()),\n            (\"body\", boxed(body)),\n        ],\n    )\n}\nfn select(source: &tir::Tir, param: &tir::LocalId, pred: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n    variant(\n        \"Select\",\n        &[\n            (\"source\", boxed(source)),\n            (\"param\", param.to_rust()),\n            (\"pred\", boxed(pred)),\n        ],\n    )\n}\nfn field(base: &tir::Tir, name: &String) -> String {\n    variant(\"Field\", &[(\"base\", boxed(base)), (\"name\", name.to_rust())])\n}\nfn builtin(which: &tir::Builtin, arg: &tir::Tir) -> String {\n    variant(\n        \"Builtin\",\n        &[(\"which\", which.to_rust()), (\"arg\", boxed(arg))],\n    )\n}\nfn index(base: &tir::Tir, index: &tir::Tir, depth: &usize, elem_is_record: &bool) -> String {\n    variant(\n        \"Index\",\n        &[\n            (\"base\", boxed(base)),\n            (\"index\", boxed(index)),\n            (\"depth\", depth.to_rust()),\n            (\"elem_is_record\", elem_is_record.to_rust()),\n        ],\n    )\n}\nfn slice(\n    base: &tir::Tir,\n    start: &Option<Box<tir::Tir>>,\n    end: &Option<Box<tir::Tir>>,\n    depth: &usize,\n) -> String {\n    variant(\n        \"Slice\",\n        &[\n            (\"base\", boxed(base)),\n            (\"start\", start.to_rust()),\n            (\"end\", end.to_rust()),\n            (\"depth\", depth.to_rust()),\n        ],\n    )\n}\nfn match_(subject: &tir::Tir, arms: &[tir::MatchArm], partial: &bool) -> String {\n    variant(\n        \"Match\",\n        &[\n            (\"subject\", boxed(subject)),\n            (\"arms\", arms.to_rust()),\n            (\"partial\", partial.to_rust()),\n        ],\n    )\n}\n\n/// Exhaustive on purpose: a `Kind` variant this does not know renders nothing rather than\n/// something wrong, and a match with no wildcard arm is what turns that into a build error the\n/// moment the checker can produce it, naming exactly the arm to add (kantord/toylang#73's\n/// \"independent of its growth\").\nimpl ToRust for tir::Kind {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        use tir::Kind::*;\n        match self {\n            Str(s) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Str({})\", s.to_rust()),\n            Int(n) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Int({})\", n.to_rust()),\n            VecLit(items) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::VecLit({})\", items.to_rust()),\n            RecordLit { fields } => record_lit(fields),\n            EnumLit { variant, payload } => enum_lit(variant, payload),\n            OptMap {\n                source,\n                param,\n                body,\n            } => opt_map(source, param, body),\n            Var(name) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Var({})\", name.to_rust()),\n            Local(id) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Local({})\", id.to_rust()),\n            Input => \"crate::tir::Kind::Input\".to_string(),\n            Inputs => \"crate::tir::Kind::Inputs\".to_string(),\n            Lines => \"crate::tir::Kind::Lines\".to_string(),\n            Dsv { delim } => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Dsv {{ delim: {} }}\", delim.to_rust()),\n            Call { func, arg } => call(func, arg),\n            Concat(a, b) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Concat({}, {})\", a.to_rust(), b.to_rust()),\n            Arith { op, lhs, rhs } => arith(op, lhs, rhs),\n            Cond {\n                cond: c,\n                then,\n                otherwise,\n            } => cond(c, then, otherwise),\n            Compare { op, lhs, rhs } => compare(op, lhs, rhs),\n            Logic { op, lhs, rhs } => logic(op, lhs, rhs),\n            Not(base) => format!(\"crate::tir::Kind::Not({})\", boxed(base)),\n            Bind { local, value, body } => bind(local, value, body),\n            Map {\n                source,\n                param,\n                body,\n            } => map(source, param, body),\n            Select {\n                source,\n                param,\n                pred,\n            } => select(source, param, pred),\n            Field { base, name } => field(base, name),\n            Builtin { which, arg } => builtin(which, arg),\n            Unwrap { base } => variant(\"Unwrap\", &[(\"base\", base.to_rust())]),\n            Index {\n                base,\n                index: i,\n                depth,\n                elem_is_record,\n            } => index(base, i, depth, elem_is_record),\n            Slice {\n                base,\n                start,\n                end,\n                depth,\n            } => slice(base, start, end, depth),\n            Match {\n                subject,\n                arms,\n                partial,\n            } => match_(subject, arms, partial),\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl ToRust for tir::Func {\n    fn to_rust(&self) -> String {\n        format!(\n            \"crate::tir::Func {{ name: {}, param: {}, param_ty: {}, body: {} }}\",\n            self.name.to_rust(),\n            self.param.to_rust(),\n            self.param_ty.to_rust(),\n            self.body.to_rust(),\n        )\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"cargo::rerun-if-changed=prelude.toy\");\n    for src in [\n        \"src/ast.rs\",\n        \"src/error.rs\",\n        \"src/parse.rs\",\n        \"src/tir.rs\",\n        \"src/ty.rs\",\n        \"src/check/mod.rs\",\n        \"src/check/types.rs\",\n        \"src/check/linearity.rs\",\n    ] {\n        println!(\"cargo::rerun-if-changed={src}\");\n    }\n\n    let src = std::fs::read_to_string(\"prelude.toy\").expect(\"prelude.toy is readable\");\n    let mut module = parse::parse_module(&src).expect(\"prelude.toy is valid toylang\");\n    // Mark prelude origin the way `prelude::module()` does, so a `pub` function may call a\n    // private helper without tripping the checker's cross-file visibility rule. Private\n    // definitions are checked here too, not just `pub` ones, so a broken helper is caught at\n    // build time rather than whenever a `pub` function first reaches it.\n    for def in &mut module.defs {\n        def.origin = ast::Origin::Prelude;\n    }\n    let module = ast::Module {\n        defs: module.defs,\n        enums: module.enums.into_iter().filter(|e| e.is_pub).collect(),\n    };\n    let funcs = check::check_module(&module).expect(\"prelude.toy checks\");\n\n    let generated = format!(\n        \"/// Every `fn` in prelude.toy, already checked, `pub` and private alike. Generated by \\\n         build.rs from prelude.toy -- edit that, not this.\\n\\\n         pub fn checked() -> Vec<crate::tir::Func> {{\\n    {}\\n}}\\n\",\n        funcs.to_rust(),\n    );\n    let out_dir = std::env::var(\"OUT_DIR\").expect(\"cargo sets OUT_DIR for a build script\");\n    std::fs::write(\n        std::path::Path::new(&out_dir).join(\"prelude_checked.rs\"),\n        generated,\n    )\n    .expect(\"can write the generated prelude source\");\n}","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":496,"totalLines":496,"truncated":false}},"title":"build.rs","time":{"start":1788292244446,"end":1788292244454}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"These tests have inline strings with fn definitions followed by the program body. They'll all need `;` added after fn definitions.\n\nThis is a big sweep. Let me now enumerate all the places with fn/type declarations:\n\n1. `src/parse.rs` — the core change.\n\n2. `src/emit_toylang.rs` — the formatter needs to emit `;` after fn/type decls (defs and aliases; modules' fns too.\n\n3. `prelude.toy` — fns need `;`.\n4. `examples/*.toy` — fns/enums... enums don't need `;`, fns do.\n5. `tests/corpus/*.yaml` — every program with a fn/type decl needs `;`.\n6. `docs/**/*.md` — every toylang fence with a fn/type decl needs `;`.\n7. `tests/*.rs` inline strings with fn/type decls need `;`.\n8. `draft.md` and other docs — hmm, draft.md is a big design doc. Let me check if it has toylang fragments. Actually the fmt harness only checks `examples/*.toy` and `docs/**/*.md` fences. draft.md is at the repo root, not under docs/. But tests/docs.rs only walks docs/. So draft.md is not checked. But wait — draft.md might be copied into docs? Let me check.\n\n\n\nActually, let me focus. The steps:\n\n1. Implement the parser/checker change. This involves:\n   - Add `Tok::Semicolon`.\n   - `def()` eats `;` after body.\n   - `alias()` eats `;` after type.\n   - Remove the same-line rule for call arguments (`takes_argument` → always true), remove `declined_cross_line` and its diagnostic.\n   - Remove the `[` same-line rule.\n   - Handle the `input` disambiguation (remove same_line from it).\n   - Keep the let-block same_line check (need `same_line` or inline).\n   - Update `emit_toylang.rs` to print `;`.\n\n2. Update prelude.toy, examples, corpus, docs, step tests.\n\n3. Run `just check`, `just fmt`, `just clippy`.\n\n4. Commit the parser/checker change FIRST as its own commit. Then docs sweep as a second commit. Actually the brief says \"Commit the parser/checker change FIRST as its own commit before starting the docs sweep\". So I need to commit the core change first. But the core change will break everything (corpus, docs, tests) until the sweep is done. So how do I commit it \"first\" if `just check` is red? The brief says \"so a partial docs sweep never strands the core change again.\" So the idea is: commit the parser/checker change (even if tests are red) as its own commit, then do the docs sweep and commit that separately. Hmm, but the brief also says \"Definition of done: implementation complete; `just check` green\". So the end state is green. The intermediate commit can be red (it's the core change). That's the point: if the docs sweep is interrupted, the core change is already committed.\n\nBut wait — can I even commit a red state? The AGENTS.md commit rules say agents may commit. The brief explicitly instructs: \"Commit the parser/checker change FIRST as its own commit before starting the docs sweep\". So yes.\n\nLet me also update the core tests (step tests, bare_application.rs) as part of the core change? Hmm. The brief says \"Commit the parser/checker change FIRST as its own commit before starting the docs sweep\". The core change includes the parser/checker and its direct tests (bare_application.rs, step tests with inline strings) and the corpus-pinned test (record_body_after_definition) and docs/reference/syntax. Hmm, actually the issue says \"Update the corpus-pinned test (record_body_after_definition) and docs/reference/syntax accordingly.\"\n\nLet me re-read the issue: \"Implementation: pick the terminator token ..., require it after every `fn` and `type` declaration, and retire the same-line same-line rule and its diagnostic once the terminator makes the ambiguity unreachable. Update the corpus-pinned test (record_body_after_definition) and docs/reference/syntax accordingly.\"\n\nSo the issue expects: parser + checker + error message, corpus-pinned test, and docs/reference/syntax.\n\n\n\nAnd the brief adds: \"Then update the docs examples and corpus... Commit the parser/checker change FIRST as its own commit before starting the docs sweep\".\n\nSo the plan:\n- Commit 1: parser/checker change + the directly-affected tests (step tests, bare_application.rs, corpus files that pin the boundary behavior, docs/reference/syntax/functions.md) + prelude.toy + examples? Hmm.\n\n\n\nActually, let me think about what \"parser/checker change\" includes. The parser change breaks every file with a fn/type declaration. So to even have a compiling project, I need to update at least prelude.toy (which is compiled at build time, via build.rs? Let me check build.rs).","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05e8623da001kXADzFIHxQBI4o","sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","messageID":"msg_05e85dcfc001LtZWMfGMidPwU3"}}
