{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788292258487,"sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_01a05e86591e7a22ad2cc872","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/src/check/mod.rs","limit":60,"offset":1520},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/src/check/mod.rs</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1520:                 let base = Tir::new(payload_ty.clone(), Kind::Local(*pid));\n1521:                 return Ok(Tir::new(\n1522:                     fty,\n1523:                     Kind::Field {\n1524:                         base: Box::new(base),\n1525:                         name: name.clone(),\n1526:                     },\n1527:                 ));\n1528:             }\n1529:             if let Some((_, t, local)) = ctx.scope.iter().rev().find(|(n, _, _)| n == name) {\n1530:                 return match local {\n1531:                     // A `let`-bound name reads the local it was bound to, not a `Var`: the\n1532:                     // backends' `Bind` only ever binds locals, so a bare name reference would dangle.\n1533: \n1534:                     Some(id) => Ok(Tir::new(t.clone(), Kind::Local(*id))),\n1535:                     None => Ok(Tir::new(t.clone(), Kind::Var(name.clone()))),\n1536:                 };\n1537:             }\n1538:             if let Some(owners) = ctx.variant_owners.get(name) {\n1539:                 let enum_ty = sole_owner(ctx, name, owners, *span)?.clone();\n1540:                 return construct(ctx, &enum_ty, name, *span, None, None);\n1541:             }\n1542:             // A function is not a value, but \"`f` is not defined\" for a defined function is a\n1543:             // lie. This is where `f -1` lands: `-` cannot start a bare argument, so the parse\n1544:             // is subtraction and `f` arrives here alone.\n1545:             if ctx.sigs.contains_key(name) || BUILTIN_NAMES.contains(&name.as_str()) {\n1546:                 return Err(Error::new(\n1547:                     *span,\n1548:                     format!(\"`{name}` is a function, not a value; write `{name}(...)` to call it\"),\n1549:                 ));\n1550:             }\n1551:             Err(Error::new(*span, format!(\"`{name}` is not defined\")))\n1552:         }\n1553: \n1554:         Expr::RecordLit { fields, .. } => {\n1555:             let mut built: Vec<(String, Tir)> = Vec::new();\n1556:             for (name, name_span, value) in fields {\n1557:                 if built.iter().any(|(seen, _)| seen == name) {\n1558:                     return Err(Error::new(\n1559:                         *name_span,\n1560:                         format!(\"field `{name}` is given twice\"),\n1561:                     ));\n1562:                 }\n1563:                 let field = synth(ctx, value)?;\n1564:                 // A stream can never enter a record: a record's fields can be printed, copied,\n1565:                 // and read back out by name, none of which make sense for a single-use stream,\n1566:                 // and no path exists for a stream-typed field to leave one again once inside.\n1567:                 if field.ty.contains_stream() {\n1568:                     return Err(Error::new(\n1569:                         value.span(),\n1570:                         format!(\"`{name}` cannot hold a stream, which has nothing to store\"),\n1571:                     ));\n1572:                 }\n1573:                 built.push((name.clone(), field));\n1574:             }\n1575:             // Declaration order, kept as written: the type's field list and this literal's\n1576:             // fields stay index-for-index aligned, which is what the columnar backends key on.\n1577:             let ty = Type::Record(\n1578:                 built\n1579:                     .iter()\n\n(Showing lines 1520-1579 of 3130. Use offset=1580 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"                let base = Tir::new(payload_ty.clone(), Kind::Local(*pid));\n                return Ok(Tir::new(\n                    fty,\n                    Kind::Field {\n                        base: Box::new(base),\n                        name: name.clone(),\n                    },\n                ));\n            }\n            if let Some((_, t, local)) = ctx.scope.iter().rev().find(|(n, _, _)| n == name) {\n                return match local {\n                    // A `let`-bound name reads the local it was bound to, not a `Var`: the\n                    // backends' `Bind` only ever binds locals, so a bare name reference would dangle.\n\n                    Some(id) => Ok(Tir::new(t.clone(), Kind::Local(*id))),\n                    None => Ok(Tir::new(t.clone(), Kind::Var(name.clone()))),\n                };\n            }\n            if let Some(owners) = ctx.variant_owners.get(name) {\n                let enum_ty = sole_owner(ctx, name, owners, *span)?.clone();","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/src/check/mod.rs","text":"                let base = Tir::new(payload_ty.clone(), Kind::Local(*pid));\n                return Ok(Tir::new(\n                    fty,\n                    Kind::Field {\n                        base: Box::new(base),\n                        name: name.clone(),\n                    },\n                ));\n            }\n            if let Some((_, t, local)) = ctx.scope.iter().rev().find(|(n, _, _)| n == name) {\n                return match local {\n                    // A `let`-bound name reads the local it was bound to, not a `Var`: the\n                    // backends' `Bind` only ever binds locals, so a bare name reference would dangle.\n\n                    Some(id) => Ok(Tir::new(t.clone(), Kind::Local(*id))),\n                    None => Ok(Tir::new(t.clone(), Kind::Var(name.clone()))),\n                };\n            }\n            if let Some(owners) = ctx.variant_owners.get(name) {\n                let enum_ty = sole_owner(ctx, name, owners, *span)?.clone();\n                return construct(ctx, &enum_ty, name, *span, None, None);\n            }\n            // A function is not a value, but \"`f` is not defined\" for a defined function is a\n            // lie. This is where `f -1` lands: `-` cannot start a bare argument, so the parse\n            // is subtraction and `f` arrives here alone.\n            if ctx.sigs.contains_key(name) || BUILTIN_NAMES.contains(&name.as_str()) {\n                return Err(Error::new(\n                    *span,\n                    format!(\"`{name}` is a function, not a value; write `{name}(...)` to call it\"),\n                ));\n            }\n            Err(Error::new(*span, format!(\"`{name}` is not defined\")))\n        }\n\n        Expr::RecordLit { fields, .. } => {\n            let mut built: Vec<(String, Tir)> = Vec::new();\n            for (name, name_span, value) in fields {\n                if built.iter().any(|(seen, _)| seen == name) {\n                    return Err(Error::new(\n                        *name_span,\n                        format!(\"field `{name}` is given twice\"),\n                    ));\n                }\n                let field = synth(ctx, value)?;\n                // A stream can never enter a record: a record's fields can be printed, copied,\n                // and read back out by name, none of which make sense for a single-use stream,\n                // and no path exists for a stream-typed field to leave one again once inside.\n                if field.ty.contains_stream() {\n                    return Err(Error::new(\n                        value.span(),\n                        format!(\"`{name}` cannot hold a stream, which has nothing to store\"),\n                    ));\n                }\n                built.push((name.clone(), field));\n            }\n            // Declaration order, kept as written: the type's field list and this literal's\n            // fields stay index-for-index aligned, which is what the columnar backends key on.\n            let ty = Type::Record(\n                built\n                    .iter()","lineStart":1520,"lineEnd":1579,"totalLines":3130,"truncated":true}},"title":"src/check/mod.rs","time":{"start":1788292258479,"end":1788292258486}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"OK, so prelude.toy is parsed at build time. So I need to update prelude.toy as part of the core change (or the build breaks).\n\nNow let me think about the overall plan and the commit structure.\n\nThe brief says: \"Commit the parser/checker change FIRST as its own commit before starting the docs sweep, so a partial docs sweep never strands the core change again.\"\n\nSo:\n- Commit 1: parser/checker change. This includes:\n  - src/parse.rs (terminator + retire same-line)\n  - src/emit_toylang.rs (formatter emits `;`)\n  - prelude.toy (needs `;` for the build to work)\n  - The directly-affected tests: bare_application.rs, step tests with inline strings, corpus files that pin the boundary (record_body_after_definition, vec_body_after_definition), and docs/reference/syntax/functions.md.\n  \n  Hmm, but wait. If I commit the parser change with only SOME of the corpus/docs updated, then `just check` will be red at that commit. That's the point of the brief — the core change is committed even if the sweep is partial. But the brief also says the end state must be green.\n\n  Actually, let me reconsider. The brief says \"Commit the parser/checker change FIRST as its own commit before starting the docs sweep\". The \"docs sweep\" is the migration of docs examples and corpus. So commit 1 = parser/checker + whatever is needed for the build to work (prelude.toy, emit_toylang). Commit 2 = the docs/corpus sweep.\n\n  But the issue also says \"Update the corpus-pinned test (record_body_after_definition) and docs/reference/syntax accordingly.\" So those are part of the core change per the issue. Hmm.\n\n  Let me think about what makes a clean commit 1. The core change is: parser requires `;`, same-line rule retired. For the project to build and the core tests to pass, I need:\n  - src/parse.rs\n  - src/emit_toylang.rs (formatter)\n  - prelude.toy\n  - The step tests and bare_application.rs that have inline fn strings (these are \"core\" tests, not docs).\n  - The corpus files (all of them, since corpus is part of `just check`).\n  - The docs fragments (part of `just check`? No — `just check` skips `every_fragment_is_a_real_program` but NOT `every_docs_fragment_is_already_formatted`).\n\nWait, let me re-read the justfile:\n```\ncheck:\n    cargo nextest run -E 'not test(every_fragment_is_a_real_program)'\n```\n\nSo `just check` runs everything EXCEPT `every_fragment_is_a_real_program` (the docs mega-test, ~137s). But it DOES run `every_docs_fragment_is_already_formatted` (the fmt_examples test) and `every_example_file_is_already_formatted`. So the docs fragments must be in canonical form for `just check` to pass.\n\nSo the \"docs sweep\" (updating docs fragments) is needed for `just check` to be green. And the corpus sweep is needed too.\n\nOK so here's my plan:\n1. Implement the core change in src/parse.rs, src/emit_toylang.rs, prelude.toy.\n2. Update the directly-affected core tests: bare_application.rs, step tests, corpus files, docs/reference/syntax/functions.md.\n3. Commit 1: the core change + core tests + prelude + formatter. (This is the \"parser/checker change\".)\n4. Commit 2: the docs sweep (all docs fragments + examples + remaining corpus).\n\nHmm, but actually — the corpus is 221 files. Most of them have fn/type declarations. The corpus is part of `just check`. So if I commit the core change without updating the corpus, `just check` is red at commit 1. That's fine per the brief — the point is the core change is committed so a partial sweep doesn't strand it.\n\nBut wait, actually, let me reconsider. Maybe the cleanest split is:\n- Commit 1: parser/checker/formatter/prelude + the tests that directly test the parser (bare_application.rs, step tests) + the corpus-pinned boundary tests (record_body_after_definition, vec_body_after_definition) + docs/reference/syntax/functions.md. This is the \"core change\" per the issue.\n- Commit 2: the full corpus + docs sweep.\n\nActually, you know what, let me not overthink the exact split. The brief says commit the parser/checker change first as its own commit. I'll do:\n- Commit 1: src/parse.rs, src/emit_toylang.rs, prelude.toy, and the tests that pin the parser behavior directly (bare_application.rs, step_*.rs inline strings, corpus boundary cases, docs/reference/syntax/functions.md). This is the core change.\n- Commit 2: the rest of the corpus + docs sweep.\n\nActually, hmm, wait. Let me reconsider. The step tests have MANY inline strings with fn declarations. And the corpus has 221 files. And docs has ~20 files with fragments. The \"core change\" commit should be focused. Let me think about what's truly \"core\":\n\nThe core change is: parser + checker + error message. The issue says \"Update the corpus-pinned test (record_body_after_definition) and docs/reference/syntax accordingly.\" So the issue itself scopes the core change to include record_body_after_definition and docs/reference/syntax.\n\nThe brief says: \"Commit the parser/checker change FIRST as its own commit before starting the docs sweep\". So the docs sweep = the migration of docs examples and corpus. The core commit = parser/checker + the issue's explicitly-named updates.\n\nBut here's the practical problem: if I commit the parser change and only update record_body_after_definition + docs/reference/syntax, then `just check` is red (all other corpus files and docs fragments break). That's acceptable per the brief — the core change is committed, and the sweep follows.\n\nBut actually, wait. There's a subtlety. If I commit the core change and it breaks the build (prelude.toy not updated), that's bad. So prelude.toy MUST be in commit 1. And emit_toylang.rs must be in commit 1 (the formatter must emit `;`).\n\nAlso, the step tests and bare_application.rs — these are Rust tests with inline toylang strings. If I don't update them in commit 1, they fail. But they're \"core\" tests. Hmm. Let me include them in commit 1 since they directly test the parser behavior (bare_application.rs especially — it tests the same-line rule which is being retired).\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole thing. The cleanest approach that satisfies the brief:\n\n**Commit 1** (\"core change\"): \n- src/parse.rs\n- src/emit_toylang.rs\n- prelude.toy\n- tests/bare_application.rs (tests the retired rule directly)\n- tests/step_*.rs (inline strings — these are the parser's own test suite)\n- tests/corpus/record_body_after_definition.yaml (the corpus-pinned test the issue names)\n- tests/corpus/vec_body_after_definition.yaml (the `[` same-line rule's pinned test)\n- docs/reference/syntax/functions.md (the issue names this)\n- Any snapshots that change as a result.\n\n**Commit 2** (\"docs sweep\"):\n- All other corpus files.\n- All docs fragments.\n- examples/*.toy.\n- site/public/corpus.json (regenerated).\n- Any remaining snapshots.\n\nHmm, but wait — the corpus files are all part of `just check`. If commit 1 leaves them broken, `just check` is red at commit 1. That's fine per the brief. But actually, let me reconsider — maybe I should just do the whole thing and commit in two logical commits. The brief's concern is that a partial docs sweep strands the core change. So as long as the core change is committed first, I'm good.\n\nActually, let me reconsider once more. There's a real risk: if I commit the core change and it's red, and then I get interrupted during the sweep, the repo is in a red state. But that's exactly what the brief wants — the core change is committed, so it's not lost. The brief explicitly says \"so a partial docs sweep never strands the core change again.\"\n\nOK. Let me also think about whether the checker needs changes. The brief says \"retiring the same-line heuristic (parser + checker + error message)\". Let me search the checker for anything related to the same-line rule or the error message.\n\nThe error message \"an argument must start on the same line as its function -- write `{callee}(...)` to call across lines\" is in parse.rs (the `unexpected` method). The checker's error message \"`{name}` is a function, not a value; write `{name}(...)` to call it\" is at check/mod.rs:1548. That's a different message (about calling a function). Let me check if that one needs changing.\n\nActually, let me re-read the issue: \"retire the same-line rule and its diagnostic\". The diagnostic is the \"an argument must start on the same line as its function\" message. That's in parse.rs. So the \"checker\" part of the brief might just be referring to the fact that the parse error message is what users see. Or maybe there's something in the checker.\n\nLet me search the checker for the \"is a function, not a value\" message and understandits context.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05e865990001fCVLxW24PYwW84","sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","messageID":"msg_05e862400001kUmzrAsS5ZvVH6"}}
