{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788292263468,"sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","part":{"id":"prt_05e866e26001UJJ08d6tkf7aHk","messageID":"msg_05e8668e90010rHCAH8TfJ0F6m","sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","snapshot":"0ebf070b795b2e106627c5d8e519929a4e05dbe7","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788292265001,"sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_01a05e86728b7dc0aa869d34","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/CONTEXT.md","limit":30,"offset":50},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/CONTEXT.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n50: also how a function takes more than one thing, because a function takes one argument and a record\n51: is how several travel as one.\n52: _Avoid_: object literal, struct literal, product literal, construction\n53: \n54: **Vec literal**:\n55: The form that builds a dimension from its entries. `[1, 2]` is one. Unlike a record literal it\n56: cannot always answer what it is, because an entry is where an element type comes from and an\n57: empty one has none.\n58: _Avoid_: array literal, list literal\n59: \n60: **Application**:\n61: Calling a function with its one argument. Two spellings, one meaning: `f x`, the bare form, is\n62: the default, and `f(x)` is the same call with the argument grouped -- the disambiguator when the\n63: bare form would read differently (an argument starting with `-`, `.`, or `[`, a compound\n64: argument, or a call spread across lines, since an argument must start on the same line as its\n65: function). Both work anywhere an atom does, including as an operand. `select` and `map` are not\n66: special syntax -- they are ordinary names reached through this same rule.\n67: _Avoid_: call, invocation, function call\n68: \n69: ### Reaching into a value\n70: \n71: **Spec**:\n72: What an access says about one dimension: keep it, narrow it, or collapse it. Every dimension of a\n73: value needs one, which is why `[]` is written rather than assumed.\n74: \n75: **Keep**:\n76: A spec that leaves a dimension at full extent, written `[]`. Streamable, since it consumes\n77: nothing.\n78: \n79: **Narrow**:\n\n(Showing lines 50-79 of 149. Use offset=80 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"also how a function takes more than one thing, because a function takes one argument and a record\nis how several travel as one.\n_Avoid_: object literal, struct literal, product literal, construction\n\n**Vec literal**:\nThe form that builds a dimension from its entries. `[1, 2]` is one. Unlike a record literal it\ncannot always answer what it is, because an entry is where an element type comes from and an\nempty one has none.\n_Avoid_: array literal, list literal\n\n**Application**:\nCalling a function with its one argument. Two spellings, one meaning: `f x`, the bare form, is\nthe default, and `f(x)` is the same call with the argument grouped -- the disambiguator when the\nbare form would read differently (an argument starting with `-`, `.`, or `[`, a compound\nargument, or a call spread across lines, since an argument must start on the same line as its\nfunction). Both work anywhere an atom does, including as an operand. `select` and `map` are not\nspecial syntax -- they are ordinary names reached through this same rule.\n_Avoid_: call, invocation, function call\n\n### Reaching into a value","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/CONTEXT.md","text":"also how a function takes more than one thing, because a function takes one argument and a record\nis how several travel as one.\n_Avoid_: object literal, struct literal, product literal, construction\n\n**Vec literal**:\nThe form that builds a dimension from its entries. `[1, 2]` is one. Unlike a record literal it\ncannot always answer what it is, because an entry is where an element type comes from and an\nempty one has none.\n_Avoid_: array literal, list literal\n\n**Application**:\nCalling a function with its one argument. Two spellings, one meaning: `f x`, the bare form, is\nthe default, and `f(x)` is the same call with the argument grouped -- the disambiguator when the\nbare form would read differently (an argument starting with `-`, `.`, or `[`, a compound\nargument, or a call spread across lines, since an argument must start on the same line as its\nfunction). Both work anywhere an atom does, including as an operand. `select` and `map` are not\nspecial syntax -- they are ordinary names reached through this same rule.\n_Avoid_: call, invocation, function call\n\n### Reaching into a value\n\n**Spec**:\nWhat an access says about one dimension: keep it, narrow it, or collapse it. Every dimension of a\nvalue needs one, which is why `[]` is written rather than assumed.\n\n**Keep**:\nA spec that leaves a dimension at full extent, written `[]`. Streamable, since it consumes\nnothing.\n\n**Narrow**:","lineStart":50,"lineEnd":79,"totalLines":149,"truncated":true}},"title":"CONTEXT.md","time":{"start":1788292264990,"end":1788292264998}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"OK. The same-line rule is in parse.rs. The checker's `check/mod.rs:1042` is about \"same linearity\" (unrelated). So the \"checker\" part of the brief is likely just loose wording, or refers to the fact that the parse error message is what the checker surfaces. The actual changes are all in parse.rs.\n\nNow, let me also check CONTEXT.md and draft.md — these are prose docs that mention the same-line rule. The issue says update docs/reference/syntax. CONTEXT.md and draft.md are design docs. Should I update them? The brief says \"update the docs examples and corpus\". CONTEXT.md and draft.md are prose, not examples. But they describe the same-line rule which is being retired. Hmm.\n\nLet me check what CONTEXT.md says and whether it's part of the docs that get tested. CONTEXT.md is at the repo root. It's not under docs/. So it's not tested by the docs harness. But it's a design doc that would now be inaccurate.\n\nLet me look at CONTEXT.md line 64 and draft.md around2023.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05e8672fe001gU2mg9b9B72j5S","sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","messageID":"msg_05e8668e90010rHCAH8TfJ0F6m"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788292265321,"sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_01a05e8673bc7b02b23b5b9b","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/draft.md","limit":40,"offset":2010},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/draft.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n2010: reproduces jq's step 4 exactly, on all six backends, verified against real jq.\n2011: \n2012: Finding the right spelling exposed a real bug rather than causing one: `jsonlines` was the first\n2013: thing to print a `Vec` of records with more than one element built directly as a literal, and\n2014: native's `vec_lit` wrote every record's whole pointer into column 0 instead of spreading its\n2015: fields across columns -- the same struct-of-arrays invariant that field access and `map` had each\n2016: already been fixed for, at a third, independent site. See\n2017: [one invariant, three independent construction sites](research-log/one-invariant-three-independent-construction-sites.md).\n2018: \n2019: ## DECIDED: `f x` reads as `f(x)`, but only where an expression begins fresh\n2020: \n2021: REVISED (2026-08-28): bare application is no longer a confined third spelling -- it is **the\n2022: default calling style**, with `f(x)` as the explicit disambiguator. Three changes carry that:\n2023: the root-position confinement and the definition-body suspension are replaced by a same-line\n2024: rule (a bare argument must start on the same line as its function -- the same rule the\n2025: record-argument sugar's boundary fix already decided, so `= extent v` works and a next-line\n2026: program body is never swallowed); the `ident {` record-argument sugar stops being separate\n2027: machinery and becomes ordinary bare application whose argument is a record atom, leaving\n2028: exactly two call forms; and the corpus, examples, and docs migrate to bare style where it\n2029: reads better, so the language teaches its own default. The `-` exclusion stands (`f -1` stays\n2030: subtraction). The section below records the original confinement and its reasoning, which the\n2031: same-line rule supersedes.\n2032: \n2033: Every function is unary, so `f(x)` never needed the parens to disambiguate which argument is\n2034: which -- only to mark where the argument starts and ends. Parens were doing two jobs: grouping\n2035: (`(x + y)`) and marking a call's boundary (`f(x)`), and those turn out to be the same job. `f(x)`\n2036: *is* `f` applied to the atom `(x)`, which happens to be a grouped `x`; nothing distinguishes it\n2037: from `f (x)`. Once seen that way, the parens around a call's argument are exactly as optional as\n2038: the parens around any other atom that does not need grouping: `f x` should mean `f(x)`.\n2039: \n2040: The obstacle was never precedence, once framed correctly. `f x + y` looks ambiguous only if the\n2041: grammar has to decide whether `+ y` extends the argument or the whole call. It does not have to\n2042: decide, because it does not have to accept the program: `x`, once taken as `f`'s bare argument,\n2043: is not an operand of anything, so `+ y` is simply left over and rejected as trailing garbage\n2044: rather than resolved either way. This is not a rule bolted on top -- it falls out of a bare call\n2045: never being reachable from `operand`'s own recursive tree (`unary`, `postfix`, `atom`), only from\n2046: `expr`'s outermost dispatch. `f(x) + y` stays legal, because the parenthesized form is an\n2047: ordinary atom, reachable from anywhere `atom` is; only the bare, parenless spelling is confined\n2048: to root position. Chaining follows the same recursion: `f g x` is `f(g(x))`, right-associative,\n2049: because `f`'s bare argument is itself allowed to be another bare call -- and, since toylang has no\n\n(Showing lines 2010-2049 of 2495. Use offset=2050 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"reproduces jq's step 4 exactly, on all six backends, verified against real jq.\n\nFinding the right spelling exposed a real bug rather than causing one: `jsonlines` was the first\nthing to print a `Vec` of records with more than one element built directly as a literal, and\nnative's `vec_lit` wrote every record's whole pointer into column 0 instead of spreading its\nfields across columns -- the same struct-of-arrays invariant that field access and `map` had each\nalready been fixed for, at a third, independent site. See\n[one invariant, three independent construction sites](research-log/one-invariant-three-independent-construction-sites.md).\n\n## DECIDED: `f x` reads as `f(x)`, but only where an expression begins fresh\n\nREVISED (2026-08-28): bare application is no longer a confined third spelling -- it is **the\ndefault calling style**, with `f(x)` as the explicit disambiguator. Three changes carry that:\nthe root-position confinement and the definition-body suspension are replaced by a same-line\nrule (a bare argument must start on the same line as its function -- the same rule the\nrecord-argument sugar's boundary fix already decided, so `= extent v` works and a next-line\nprogram body is never swallowed); the `ident {` record-argument sugar stops being separate\nmachinery and becomes ordinary bare application whose argument is a record atom, leaving\nexactly two call forms; and the corpus, examples, and docs migrate to bare style where it\nreads better, so the language teaches its own default. The `-` exclusion stands (`f -1` stays","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-153/draft.md","text":"reproduces jq's step 4 exactly, on all six backends, verified against real jq.\n\nFinding the right spelling exposed a real bug rather than causing one: `jsonlines` was the first\nthing to print a `Vec` of records with more than one element built directly as a literal, and\nnative's `vec_lit` wrote every record's whole pointer into column 0 instead of spreading its\nfields across columns -- the same struct-of-arrays invariant that field access and `map` had each\nalready been fixed for, at a third, independent site. See\n[one invariant, three independent construction sites](research-log/one-invariant-three-independent-construction-sites.md).\n\n## DECIDED: `f x` reads as `f(x)`, but only where an expression begins fresh\n\nREVISED (2026-08-28): bare application is no longer a confined third spelling -- it is **the\ndefault calling style**, with `f(x)` as the explicit disambiguator. Three changes carry that:\nthe root-position confinement and the definition-body suspension are replaced by a same-line\nrule (a bare argument must start on the same line as its function -- the same rule the\nrecord-argument sugar's boundary fix already decided, so `= extent v` works and a next-line\nprogram body is never swallowed); the `ident {` record-argument sugar stops being separate\nmachinery and becomes ordinary bare application whose argument is a record atom, leaving\nexactly two call forms; and the corpus, examples, and docs migrate to bare style where it\nreads better, so the language teaches its own default. The `-` exclusion stands (`f -1` stays\nsubtraction). The section below records the original confinement and its reasoning, which the\nsame-line rule supersedes.\n\nEvery function is unary, so `f(x)` never needed the parens to disambiguate which argument is\nwhich -- only to mark where the argument starts and ends. Parens were doing two jobs: grouping\n(`(x + y)`) and marking a call's boundary (`f(x)`), and those turn out to be the same job. `f(x)`\n*is* `f` applied to the atom `(x)`, which happens to be a grouped `x`; nothing distinguishes it\nfrom `f (x)`. Once seen that way, the parens around a call's argument are exactly as optional as\nthe parens around any other atom that does not need grouping: `f x` should mean `f(x)`.\n\nThe obstacle was never precedence, once framed correctly. `f x + y` looks ambiguous only if the\ngrammar has to decide whether `+ y` extends the argument or the whole call. It does not have to\ndecide, because it does not have to accept the program: `x`, once taken as `f`'s bare argument,\nis not an operand of anything, so `+ y` is simply left over and rejected as trailing garbage\nrather than resolved either way. This is not a rule bolted on top -- it falls out of a bare call\nnever being reachable from `operand`'s own recursive tree (`unary`, `postfix`, `atom`), only from\n`expr`'s outermost dispatch. `f(x) + y` stays legal, because the parenthesized form is an\nordinary atom, reachable from anywhere `atom` is; only the bare, parenless spelling is confined\nto root position. Chaining follows the same recursion: `f g x` is `f(g(x))`, right-associative,\nbecause `f`'s bare argument is itself allowed to be another bare call -- and, since toylang has no","lineStart":2010,"lineEnd":2049,"totalLines":2495,"truncated":true}},"title":"draft.md","time":{"start":1788292265273,"end":1788292265279}},"id":"prt_05e86742e001ADxqpacMFgRr5B","sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","messageID":"msg_05e8668e90010rHCAH8TfJ0F6m"}}
