{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788292425914,"sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"todowrite","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-ab1972956a3eae90","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"todos":[{"content":"Implement core parser change: add ; token, require terminator after fn/type decls, retire same-line rule and diagnostic","status":"in_progress","priority":"high"},{"content":"Update emit_toylang.rs formatter to emit ; after fn/type decls","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Update prelude.toy with terminators","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Commit core change (parser/checker/formatter/prelude + direct tests) as first commit","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Sweep corpus YAML files with terminators (use toylang fmt for canonical form)","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Sweep docs fragments and examples with terminators (run toylang fmt)","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Regenerate site/public/corpus.json and snapshots","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Run just check, just fmt, just clippy; commit docs sweep","status":"pending","priority":"high"}]},"output":"[\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Implement core parser change: add ; token, require terminator after fn/type decls, retire same-line rule and diagnostic\",\n    \"status\": \"in_progress\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Update emit_toylang.rs formatter to emit ; after fn/type decls\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Update prelude.toy with terminators\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Commit core change (parser/checker/formatter/prelude + direct tests) as first commit\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Sweep corpus YAML files with terminators (use toylang fmt for canonical form)\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Sweep docs fragments and examples with terminators (run toylang fmt)\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Regenerate site/public/corpus.json and snapshots\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"content\": \"Run just check, just fmt, just clippy; commit docs sweep\",\n    \"status\": \"pending\",\n    \"priority\": \"high\"\n  }\n]","metadata":{"todos":[{"content":"Implement core parser change: add ; token, require terminator after fn/type decls, retire same-line rule and diagnostic","status":"in_progress","priority":"high"},{"content":"Update emit_toylang.rs formatter to emit ; after fn/type decls","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Update prelude.toy with terminators","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Commit core change (parser/checker/formatter/prelude + direct tests) as first commit","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Sweep corpus YAML files with terminators (use toylang fmt for canonical form)","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Sweep docs fragments and examples with terminators (run toylang fmt)","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Regenerate site/public/corpus.json and snapshots","status":"pending","priority":"high"},{"content":"Run just check, just fmt, just clippy; commit docs sweep","status":"pending","priority":"high"}],"truncated":false},"title":"8 todos","time":{"start":1788292425909,"end":1788292425911}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"These prose docs (draft.md, CONTEXT.md, plans/language-oddities.md) describe the same-line rule. The issue says update docs/reference/syntax. It doesn't explicitly mention CONTEXT.md or draft.md. But since they now describe a retired rule, they'd be inaccurate.\n\nHowever, the brief says \"do not touch plans/\". So I must NOT touch plans/ (including plans/language-oddities.md). \n\nFor CONTEXT.md and draft.md: these are design/prose docs. They're not part of the tested docs. The issue's scope is \"Update the corpus-pinned test (record_body_after_definition) and docs/reference/syntax accordingly.\" \n\nHmm. Should I update CONTEXT.md and draft.md? They're accurate descriptions of the language as it was. After my change, they'd be inaccurate. But the brief says focus on the ruled change and the docs examples and corpus sweep. draft.md is a huge design doc. Updating it is risky and out of scope.\n\nLet me think about the \"Authority ordering\" and \"provenance\" in AGENTS.md. CONTEXT.md and draft.md are human-authored design docs. Changing them should be a human's call, or at least carefully considered. The brief doesn't ask me to update them. The brief says \"update the docs examples and corpus\". \n\nActually, let me re-read the brief: \"Then update the docs examples and corpus: the KNOWN TRAP is that a migration script which rewrites fences tends to re-indent fragment bodies from 4 spaces to 8, which fails the docs fmt_examples canonical-form test on 32 fragments across ~20 files -- avoid that entirely by running toylang fmt on each updated fragment so the canonical form is produced by the formatter, never by hand or by sed.\"\n\nSo the docs sweep = docs examples (fences) and corpus. That's the scope. CONTEXT.md and draft.md are not examples or corpus. I'll leave them, but I should note the same-line rule retirement in docs/reference/syntax/functions.md (which the issue names).\n\nActually wait — let me reconsider. The issue says \"retire the same-line rule and its diagnostic once the terminator makes the ambiguity unreachable. Update the corpus-pinned test (record_body_after_definition) and docs/reference/syntax accordingly.\"\n\nSo docs/reference/syntax is explicitly in scope. Let me update functions.md and programs.md (and maybe types/alias.md) as needed. The brief says the \"docs sweep\" — I'll include the reference docs that describe declarations.\n\nLet me now check: does the docs/reference describe the terminator anywhere? Let me search docs for fn/type declaration syntax examples that need `;`.\n\nOK, I think I have enough understanding. Let me also check whether there's an ESCALATION.md currently present, and check git state.\n\nLet me now start implementing. First, let me set up a todo list.\n\nLet me reconsider the scope of the core change once more and the same-line rule removal details.\n\nIn parse.rs:\n1. Add `Tok::Semicolon`, lex `;`.\n2. `def()`: after `let body = self.def_body()?;`, add `let terminator = self.eat(Tok::Semicolon)?;`. But wait — the `span` of the Def uses `body.span()`. Should it include the semicolon? Probably keep it as body span, or include the terminator. Doesn't matter much for the Def span (used for ordering). Let me include it for correctness. Actually the span is `start.to(body.span())`. I'll keep that.\n\nActually, wait. There's a subtlety with `def_body`. If the body is a `let` block, the let-block is line-structured. After the let-block body, we need `;`. That's fine.\n\n3. `alias()`: after `let ty = self.type_expr()?;`, add `self.eat(Tok::Semicolon)?;`.\n\n4. `enum_decl()`: no `;` needed (ends with `}`). Leave as is.\n\n5. In `parse()`: the defs loop reads `fn`/`pub`/`type`/`enum`. After each, the `;` is consumed inside `def()`/`alias()`. The loop continues. Enums don't consume `;`. So after an enum, the next token is the next declaration. Good.\n\nWait, but there's a subtlety. The defs loop breaks when it sees a token that's not fn/pub/type/enum. After the last declaration, the `;` has been consumed. So the loop continues and sees the next token. If that's `input`, it breaks (good — the input handling follows). If that's the body start, it breaks. Good.\n\n6. The `input <type>` handling: remove `&& p.same_line(input_span.end, next_span.start)`. So `input` followed by `{` or `Ident` is a declaration. But wait — should the `input` declaration also get a `;`? The issue says only fn and type. But hmm, let me reconsider. If `input {x: Int}` doesn't get a `;`, then after reading the type `{x: Int}`, we proceed to the body. The type is self-delimiting (`}`). Good. But consider `input Int` followed by the body on the next line. `Int` is a named type. Then the body starts. Good.\n\nActually wait — should I reconsider whether `input <type>` needs a terminator? Let me think about a tricky case:\n```\ninput Int\nInt\n```\nHere `input Int` is a declaration (input declared as Int). Then body = `Int`... but `Int` is a type, not a value. So it fails in the checker. That's fine.\n\nWhat about:\n```\ninput Int\n1\n```\n`input Int` declaration, body = `1`. Valid? input is declared as Int, and the body `1` doesn't use input. Hmm, that's a program that reads input but never uses it. Actually the input declaration declares the type; if the body doesn't use input, is that an error? Let me not worry — that's existing behavior, not affected by the terminator.\n\nActually, hmm — wait. Let me reconsider whether the `input` declaration should get a `;`. If it doesn't, there's a potential ambiguity: `input Int` followed by a body that starts with something that looks like it continues the type. But types can't be continued by arbitrary expressions. `input Vec<Int>` — after `>`, the body starts. Fine.\n\nBut actually, there's a subtle issue. Consider a program where the body itself is a record literal and input is declared:\n```\ninput {x: Int}\n{x: 1}\n```\nWait, that's `input {x: Int}` declaration, then body `{x: 1}`. But `input {x: Int}` — the type is `{x: Int}` which ends with `}`. Then body `{x: 1}`. That works. But hold on — how does the parser know that `{x: Int}` is the input's type and not the start of the body? Because `input` followed by `{` triggers the declaration. After reading the record type `{x: Int}`, the parser moves to the body. The body is `{x: 1}`. Good.\n\nOK so `input <type>` doesn't strictly need a `;`. But wait — should I reconsider adding a `;` to input for consistency and safety? The issue explicitly says \"require it after every `fn` and `type` declaration\". So I'll follow that literally and NOT add `;` to `input`. Actually, hmm, let me reconsider. The `input` declaration IS a \"declaration\" in the general sense. But the issue specifically scopes to fn and type. And the input declaration is self-delimiting by its type (which is always a complete type expr). So no `;` needed.\n\nBut actually, wait. Let me reconsider the input disambiguation once more, because removing the same-line check could create a NEW ambiguity. Consider:\n```\nfn f(x: Int) -> Int = x;\n\n{a: f(input), b: input}\n```\nAfter `fn f(...) = x;`, the defs loop sees `{`. Breaks. Then input check: next token is `{`, not `input`. So no input declaration. Body = `{a: f(input), b: input}`. Good.\n\nAnd:\n```\nfn f(x: Int) -> Int = x;\n\ninput {x: Int}\nf(input)\n```\nAfter `f(...) = x;`, defs loop sees `input`. Breaks. Input check: next is `input`, next2 is `{`. So declaration. `input {x: Int}` read. Then body = `f(input)`. Good.\n\nNow, is there a case where removing the same-line check for input causes a problem? Consider a program where input is used as a VALUE and it's the FIRST token of the body, followed by a record literal on the next line:\n```\nfn f(x: Int) -> Int = x;\n\ninput\n{a: 1}\n```\nCurrently (with same-line): `input` followed by `{` on the next line → NOT same line → not a declaration → body = `input`, then `{a: 1}` → parse error \"expected end of program, found `{`\".\n\nAfter removing same-line: `input` followed by `{` → declaration `input {a: 1}`. But `1` is not a type → parse error \"expected a type, found `1`\". \n\nSo the program `input\\n{a: 1}` is invalid either way, just a different error message. No valid program affected.\n\nHmm, but wait. What about `input` as a value where the body is `input` and then nothing? That works (bare_input). And `input` as a value followed by `f`? \n```\nfn f(x: Int) -> Int = x;\n\ninput\nf(input)\n```\nHmm, this is `input` (value) followed by `f(input)` (another expr). Two expressions → invalid. After change: `input f` → declaration `input <type f>`, then body `(input)`. The checker: `f` is a function, not a type → error. Either way invalid.\n\nOK so removing the same-line check for input is safe.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider — is it possible that removing the same-line check breaks the case where `input` is used as a value in the body AND there happens to be a `{` or Ident after it in the same expression? Like the body is a pipeline `input | map(...)`. `input` followed by `|`. Not `{` or Ident. Fine.\n\nOr the body is a call `f(input)`. `input` is inside parens, never the first token of the body. Fine.\n\nOK. Now, let me reconsider whether the input handling even needs the `{`/Ident check at all, or whether I should make it more robust. Actually the current logic is:\n```rust\nif (next == Tok::LBrace || matches!(next, Tok::Ident(_))) { ... declaration ... }\n```\nI'll just remove the same_line condition, keeping the `{`/Ident check. This means: `input` followed by `{` or an identifier is a declaration (regardless of line). Otherwise it's the start of the body.\n\nHmm wait, but there's actually a real concern I missed. What about a program where the body is `input` used as a value, and the body ALSO has an `input <type>` declaration? No, you can't have both — the declaration is read first, and then `input` in the body is the value.\n\nWhat about: the body is just `input` and there's a `fn` with `input` in it? Like:\n```\nfn f(x: Int) -> Int = x;\n\ninput\n```\nBody = `input`. Fine.\n\nOK. Let me now also reconsider: should I keep the `same_line` method for the let-block rule? Yes. The let-block rule (issue #87) is a separate line-structured rule. I'll keep `same_line` and the `src` field.\n\nWait, but actually — hold on. Let me reconsider whether retiring the same-line rule for call arguments interacts badly with the let-block rule. In `def_body`, the let-block uses `same_line(binding_start, value.span().end)`. That's about a binding's value fitting on its line. Unrelated to call arguments. Keep it.\n\nNow, let me also reconsider the `[` indexing same-line rule. I decided to retire it. But wait — let me reconsider whether that's actually safe with respect to the `input` declaration and the body.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether I should retire the `[` rule or keep it. Let me re-read the issue once more: \"retire the same-line rule and its diagnostic\". The `[` rule is a distinct rule (issue #148) that uses the same `same_line` mechanism. \n\nHmm. Let me think about the cleanest design. The brief says \"retiring the same-line heuristic (parser + checker + error message)\". The \"heuristic\" is the line-position-based disambiguation. The `[` rule IS part of that heuristic (it's line-position based). So I should retire it too.\n\nBut the side effect: `[1, 2]\\n[0]` becomes `[1,2][0]` = 2 instead of a parse error. Let me check if any corpus case or docs fragment relies on the `[` same-line behavior (i.e., has an expression followed by `[` on a different line that should NOT be an index). Given the formatter always writes `v[...]` on one line, and vec literals after a def are now terminated by `;`, I don't think any case relies on it. Let me verify during the sweep.\n\nActually, wait — let me reconsider. There's a case that could matter: a multi-line vec literal body after a definition. E.g.:\n```\nfn f() -> Vec<Int> = [1, 2]\n\n[3, 4]\n```\nWith terminator: `[1, 2];` then body `[3, 4]`. Fine.\n\nBut what about a def body that is itself a multi-line vec literal, followed by another statement? No, defs end with `;`.\n\nWhat about inside a single expression where a vec literal is on the next line after something? Like:\n```\n[1, 2]\n| map(. + 1)\n```\nHere `[1, 2]` followed by `|` on the next line. `|` is a pipe, not `[`. Fine.\n\nOK, retiring the `[` rule should be fine. Let me do it.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider ONE more time. The concern with retiring the `[` rule: does it break the `postfix` handling of indexing in a way that matters? Currently:\n```rust\nTok::LBracket => {\n    let (_, bspan) = self.peek()?;\n    if !self.same_line(e.span().end, bspan.start) {\n        return Ok(e);\n    }\n    ...\n}\n```\nIf I remove the same-line check, then `[` always starts an index/project/slice. That's the pre-#148 behavior. The formatter will render `v[0]` etc. Fine.\n\nBut hold on — there's a specific concern. The `vec_body_after_definition` corpus case:\n```\nfn user_fn(x: Int) -> Int = x * 2\n\n[user_fn(1), user_fn(2)]\n```\nWith terminator: `fn user_fn(x: Int) -> Int = x * 2;` then body `[user_fn(1), user_fn(2)]`. Fine. The `[` here is the START of the body, so it's parsed as a vec literal (atom), not an index. Good.\n\nOK. Now, one more consideration: after retiring the same-line rule for call arguments, what happens to the `declined_cross_line` field and the `unexpected` enrichment? I need to remove:\n- The `declined_cross_line` field.\n- The enrichment in `unexpected`.\n- The `takes_argument` method (replace with a simple check).\n- Update call sites of `takes_argument`.\n\n`takes_argument` is called in `ident_expr` (for variant payloads) and for the argument. Currently:\n```rust\nlet (payload, end) = if (after == Tok::LParen || after == Tok::LBrace)\n    && self.takes_argument(&format!(\"{name}.{variant}\"), variant_span, after_span)\n{ ... }\n```\nand\n```rust\nif !argument_starts || !self.takes_argument(&name, span, next_span) {\n    return Ok(Expr::Var { name, span });\n}\n```\n\nIf I retire the same-line rule, `takes_argument` always returns true. So I can remove the `takes_argument` calls entirely (just check `argument_starts`). And for the variant, just check `after == LParen || LBrace`.\n\nLet me now write the parser changes.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the `unexpected` method. It's used to enrich errors when a call was declined across lines. If I remove `declined_cross_line`, the `unexpected` method just returns `Error::new(span, msg)`. So I can simplify it. But `unexpected` is called in many places. Let me keep `unexpected` as a plain wrapper (or just inline). Actually, let me keep `unexpected` but remove the enrichment. That minimizes changes to call sites.\n\nWait, but if I remove the `declined_cross_line` field and the enrichment, then `unexpected` becomes:\n```rust\nfn unexpected(&self, span: Span, msg: String) -> Error {\n    Error::new(span, msg)\n}\n```\nThat's a trivial wrapper. I could keep it for minimal diff, or inline it. Let me keep it to minimize changes (it's called in ~6 places).\n\nHmm, but then `src` field is only used by `same_line` for the let-block rule. Keep `src`.\n\nLet me now also reconsider: the `same_line` method is used in `def_body` (let-block) and was used in `takes_argument` and `[` indexing and `input`. After my changes, `same_line` is only used in `def_body`. Keep it.\n\nNow let me write the changes to parse.rs.\n\nLet me first make the edit to add the Semicolon token.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05e88daff001Bs5e550E7pmDT2","sessionID":"ses_fa17fa8b8ffeRDvLpqU7vbxt5Y","messageID":"msg_05e86757b0015rWwsnquqUFwse"}}
